\par }{\fs28\cf12 Introduction}{\fs24 \tab Welcome to Player\rquote s Option }{\i\fs24 Skills & Powers}{\fs24 , the companion to the Player\rquote s Option}{\i\fs24  Combat & Tactics}{\fs24  book. This volume unveils new role\_playing possibilitie
s without increasing the complexity of the AD&D\'ae game. Just as the }{\i\fs24 Combat & Tactics}{\fs24  book widened the scope of encounters, this tome expands character creation. Together, these books heighten players\rquote 
 involvement with their characters, the campaign, and the AD&D game as a whole. Use what you like, change what you don\rquote t like, and above all have fun!
\par }{\fs28\cf12 \tab }{\fs24 The chapters in this book are building blocks for constructing Player\rquote s Option characters. AD&D game players will find several familiar terms, such as kits. But 
these kits are different than those presented in supplements such as }{\i\fs24 The Complete Fighter\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24 . Instead, Player\rquote 
s Option kits are packages coupled with special skills that add depth and flavor to characters. There are other changes and additions, and
 these will unfold as you read this book. One such change is selecting character abilities using a character point system. Here\rquote s an overview of what character points do:
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Character Points}{\fs24  Every character begins play with a number of character points deter
mined by his race and class. Further, every time a character advances a level, he gains 3 to 5 character points. The character point system is a new concept for the AD&D game. The points serve numerous purposes, and these are summarized below. Note: This 
book assumes that a typical campaign will award 3 CPs per level, and that most characters will use one of these during play (see below). Larger character point awards will result in a higher\_powered campaign, but if both DM and players agree, that
\rquote s fine\emdash enjoy yourselves. 
\par 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}
\ls1\adjustright {\fs24 Character points are used to purchase weapon proficiencies. New weapon proficiencies usually cost 2 or 3 character points. This is discussed in chapter seven.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}
\ls1\adjustright {\fs24 Similarly, character points are used to purchase nonweapon proficiencies.
 Purchasing a nonweapon proficiency usually costs from 2 to 5 points. Also, character points are used to purchase traits, and bonus character points are awarded for taking disadvantages. This is discussed in chapter six.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}
\ls1\adjustright {\fs24 Points can be used to increase p
roficiency scores. One character point improves a proficiency score by a +1 permanent bonus. For example, A wizard with an Intelligence of 17 has a spellcraft proficiency score of 11. The character will successfully use this proficiency on a d20 roll of 1
1 or less. The wizard could improve this score from 11 to 12 by spending 1 character point. This is covered in chapter six.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}
\ls1\adjustright {\fs24 Points can be spent to acquire racial abilities for demihumans, such as infravision or the elves\rquote  attack bonus with swords or bows
. Racial abilities cost 5 to 10 character points. Humans have no racial abilities and can spend their points in other ways. This is discussed in chapter three.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}
\ls1\adjustright {\fs24 Player characters can spend points on acquiring class abilities, such as the paladin\rquote s capacity to}{\i\fs24  lay on hands}{\fs24 
. Each class ability usually costs 10 character points. There is more information about this in chapter four.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}
\ls1\adjustright {\fs24 Priests and wizards can spend points to gain extra spells. Only one additional enchantment per level can be purchased
 this way, and the cost is 2 points plus 1 point per level of the spell purchased. Priests and wizards cannot purchase higher level spells than they can cast. Look to chapters four and eight for more information on magic and character points.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}
\ls1\adjustright {\fs24 Points can be spent to improve a character\rquote 
s roll for additional hit points when advancing a level. For every 2 character points spent, the player can roll one additional die when determining new hit points, taking the highest number from the dice rolled.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}
\ls1\adjustright {\fs24 Charact
er points also can be used in the course of play, not just during character creation or between adventures. During a game session, a player can spend saved points to give his character a second chance to accomplish a feat, or to reroll a failed attack, sa
ving throw, proficiency check, or even a low damage roll.\tab 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab 
\par \tab For example, Leon chooses to save 2 character points after creating his fighter. During a game, Leon\rquote s fighter tries to strike an ogre. The attack roll fails. However, the ogre strikes the fighte
r, wounding him badly. In the next round, fearing for his fighter\rquote 
s life, Leon declares that he will spend a character point if his attack misses. The roll indeed fails, and Leon now can spend a point and reroll his attack. If this attack roll succeeds, the first roll is ignored and Leon\rquote 
s fighter has struck the ogre. If the reroll failed, Leon\rquote s character still misses and play continues.\tab 
\par   Regardless of any reroll\rquote s success, any character points spent are lost. Players can continue to spend points as long as they declare so before rolling any dice\emdash 
and as long as they have points to spend. This mechanic allows characters a second chance to accomplish important tasks or to achieve feats vital to the story.
\par 
\par \tab More information about character points can be found throughout this volume. The following section discusses establishing character statistics.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Character Generation}{\fs24   The }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24  details several methods for determining a character\rquote s six ability scores. These are summarized below, in addition to 
a few new ideas that allow players more control over their characters. DMs should review all the options and choose one for their campaign so that all characters are created using the same method.
\par \tab The six systems of character creation from the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24 
 generate different types of heroes. Methods I and III yield characters with few scores of 15 or above. These methods are best for campaigns in which the heroes arise from the common people. These characters usually have average ability scores, and a
re often less capable of amazing feats. Low\_fantasy or low\_magic campaigns with a grim or gritty tone are the perfect settings for such characters.
\par \tab However, if epic story lines filled with rousing adventure and swashbuckling action are preferred\emdash and the characters are expected to perform three miraculous quests before breakfast\emdash 
higher ability scores are called for. Characters generated using Method IV are particularly suited for this type of campaign. The other generation methods give varying results.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Method I:}{\fs24  Roll 3d6 for each of a character\rquote s six ability scores and record the dice total in the following order: Strength, Dexterity, Constitution, Intelligence, Wisdom, and Charisma.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Method II:}{\fs24  Roll 3d6 twice for each ability. Choose the highest total of each pair of rolls and record the scores in the order: Strength, Dexterity, Constitution, Intelligence, Wisdom, and Charisma.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Method III:}{\fs24  Roll 3d6 six times, noting each total. Players place the totals in the abilities of their choice.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Method IV:}{\fs24  Roll 3d6 12 times and note the totals. Players can choose the six highest totals and assign those six to abilities in any order they want.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Method V:}{\fs24  Players roll 4d6 for each ability score and select the three highest numbers. Then the numbers are assigned to characters\rquote  abilities as desired.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Method VI:}{\fs24  All six abilities begin with a score of 8. The player has an additional 7d6 to divide among those scores. All the points from a d6 must be applied to one ability score. Scores of 18 are possible only if the total
 of one or more dice, added to the base of 8, add up to exactly 18. Scores higher than 18 are not possible\emdash except in some campaigns, such as the Dark Sun\'ae setting.
\par 
