\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Reaction Adjustment:}{\fs24 
 This number modifies the Reaction Roll made when a character interacts with NPCs and intelligent creatures for the first time (see the DMG). Obnoxious behavior can negate bonuses for a high Appearance score, just as solicitous manners 
can overcome a weakness.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Proficiencies:}{\fs24  This score acts as the base number for proficiencies such as dancing and etiquette.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Alternate Subability Method
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab Rather than simply assigning numbers to subability scores, players can determine the scores randomly. First, generate the basic ability score; for example, Leon rolls 3d6 for his character\rquote 
s Strength score and gets a 13. Second, the player assigns the base score to one of the subability scores; Leon assigns his roll of 13 to his character\rquote 
s Strength/Stamina score. Third, roll a die or flip a coin to see if the remaining subability score will be better or worse than the base score. Leon rolls 1d6 and gets a 4, so his character\rquote 
s Strength/Muscle score will be higher than Strength/Stamina. Last, add or subtract 1d4 to the base score to determine the what the second subability actually is; Leon rolls a 3, so his character\rquote s Strength/Muscle score is 16.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Subability Checks}{\fs24   Subabilities are more specific definitions of a character\rquote 
s aptitudes. They can be used more often to determine the success of actions beyond simple proficiency checks. The concept of ability checks was introduced in the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24 . This 
concept is expanded with subabilities. Specific examples of the various subability checks follow. The lists are not intended to be all\_encompassing, but simply guidelines of the uses of subability checks.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Stamina:}{\fs24  This subability is used to ascertain a character\rquote 
s physical exertion over a period of time. Sample opportunities to call for Stamina checks include a character holding a heavy weight or propping up a collapsing ceiling. When a character is in pursuit of an NPC or monster that has the same moveme
nt rate, the Stamina check determines who will tire first. Other checks could involve long\_
term physical exertion such as swimming vast distances, defending the castle walls from attackers over the course of hours or days, or staying awake long enough to memorize spells for the next day\emdash after a long day of adventuring.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Muscle:}{\fs24  Times to call for Muscle checks include when characters try to pick up heavy objects, leap chasms, arm wrestle, and display feats of strength.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Aim:}{\fs24  Potential times to use Aim checks include when characters enter games of skill, such as darts; when they try sleight\_of\_hand maneuvers; and when they try to catch things thrown at them, such as balls, coins, or books.

\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Balance:}{\fs24  Balance checks can be called for when a character walks along a narrow ledge to reach a window, when he flees across a rope bridge while trying to avoid attackers\rquote 
 missile weapons, and when he catches a handhold after an enemy damages that rope bridge and causes it to collapse.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Health:}{\fs24  Health checks can be used to determine a character\rquote s resistance to a disease, and to find out if a character catches the flu going around the kingdom. They also determine a character\rquote 
s resistance to intoxicants, drugs, or poisons.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Fitness:}{\fs24  Fitness checks can be used to gauge an adventurer\rquote s resistance to longer\_term hardships such as forced marches, food or water deprivation, and non\_lethal torture.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Reason:}{\fs24  Good times to call for Reason checks include when a character is searching for clue
s at the scene of a crime, when someone is trying to learn new information, or when a character is attempting to read a code or decipher a cryptic message.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Knowledge:}{\fs24  Knowledge checks can be used when characters try to understand a strange language or recall bits of information they have seemingly forgotten.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Intuition:}{\fs24  These checks are called for when adventurers think they are being followed, that their NPC friend is acting strangely, or when someone attempts to con them.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Willpower:}{\fs24  Willpower checks are needed when a character\rquote 
s force of will is challenged, such as when he is offered a bribe, is interrogated, or is blackmailed. Other possibilities include when a character tries to persuade an NPC about an important issue or point, or when a character is 
tortured to surrender valuable information.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Leadership:}{\fs24  Situations calling for Leadership checks include when an adventurer leads NPCs into battle, when one adventurer asks a favor of another, or when a character attempts to instill a belief or attitude i
nto a crowd or mob.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Appearance:}{\fs24  Appearance checks can be used in social circumstances where the involved character has no established reputation. Or the checks might determine if an NPC becomes romantically interested in the character.
\par 
\par One problem with ability checks as they now stand follows:
\par   \ldblquote Leon, see if your 17 Muscle fighter can lift that iron chest.\rdblquote 
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 (Rolling a d20)}{\fs24  \ldblquote Missed it. I rolled a 19.\rdblquote 
\par \tab \ldblquote Tomas, check if your 6 Muscle wizard can do it.\rdblquote 
\par \tab \ldblquote Yes!\emdash I made it with a 4!\rdblquote 
\par 
\par \tab The above reflects that
 sometimes a character with a high score will fail, only to have a character with a lower score get lucky and succeed. Depending on the number of points in each subability score, the character can make additional attempts at feats. The rule is: for each p
oint in a subability above 15, the player can roll an additional 1d20 for checks against that subability. For example, because Leon\rquote s fighter has a 17 Muscle, the character can make up to three checks\emdash 
the initial check, plus two more. So if Leon\rquote s fighter wants to force open a trap door, Leon rolls 3d20. If any of the dice register a successful check, the fighter opens the door. Tomas\rquote  character, with a 6 Muscle only rolls 1d20.
\par \tab This multiple d20 system does not eliminate the possibility of a character with a low ability score succeeding where a character with a higher ability score fails. But the system does reduce the number of times such an improbable incident will occur.

\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Heightened Difficulties}{\fs24   An additional benefit of this system is that the DM can set particularly difficult checks for characters with high ability scores\emdash requiring two or more successes on the characters\rquote 
 multiple d20 rolls.
\par \tab For example, if the iron chest Leon\rquote s fighter wants to lift had been welded in place, the DM could claim that two or even all three of the fighter\rquote 
s d20 rolls be successful for the chest to come free. This further reinforces the idea that characters with low scores are not as capable as their comrades with higher scores.
\par \tab For those occasions when a lone adventurer cannot succeed at an ability check due to insufficient dice, additional characters might be able to help. If the DM rules that more than one character can attempt the feat, roll the d20s of the cha
racter with the highest ability score, then add 1d20 for each additional character assisting the primary character.
\par \tab This system also can be used to determine the outcome of direct ability\_vs.\_ability contests between characters. The winner of such a contest is the one who successfully rolls the highest result\emdash 
if that result is equal to or under the ability score in question. For example, if two characters with 14 Aim scores compete to see who can hit a far\_off boulder with thrown stones\emdash and the results of the d20 rolls are a 14 and a 6\emdash 
the character who rolled the 14 wins the contest and strikes the boulder first. Both characters\rquote  rolls succeeded, but the 14 is a higher degree of success than the 6. If the character with the 14 result had instead rolled a 1
5 or a 5, the other competitor would have won, in the first case because 15 is not a successful Aim check, and in the second because a 6 is higher than a 5.
\par \tab Another common example of such a contest is an arm\_wrestling match. Marshall has a ranger with a S
trength of 15 and subability scores of Stamina 13 and Muscle 17. Brian has a fighter with a Strength of 13 and subability scores of 13 in both Stamina and Muscle. These characters decide to arm wrestle to see who gets the first choice of treasure. The fir
st ability check would be against Muscle to see if either character could overwhelm the other with a burst of Strength. Marshall and Brian roll 1d20, and both Muscle check results are 11. The characters have matched power for power.
\par \tab The next check (and all subsequent checks) would be against Stamina to see which character tires first. Marshall rolls a 7 and Brian rolls a 12. Both are successes, but Brian\rquote s roll was higher. Brian\rquote s character wins the arm\_
wrestling match despite having lower Strength and Muscle scores. Simply put, Brian\rquote s character got lucky, and the next match between the characters could turn out differently.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Abilties vs. Thief Skills}{\fs24   A note should be made regarding ability checks that mirror the class abilities of rogues. Characters with 
high Balance scores may, by the numbers, seem to be better at thieving skills than some rogues, especially low\_
level ones. DMs should take care not to let nonrogue characters steal the spotlight away from the real rogues. The climb walls ability is a good 
example. Most characters with above average Balance scores would have a relatively simple time climbing a craggy rock face or cliff, but only thieves can climb brick walls or sheer surfaces.
\par \tab DMs who wish to grant characters with high ability scores some chance of success at feats similar to thieving abilities could require multiple successes for such characters to succeed.
\par 
