\par }{\fs28\cf12 Character Points}{\fs24   Each race detailed below receives a number of character points that can be spent o
n racial skills and other abilities. This lets players personalize their characters. No longer must all dwarves have exactly the same abilities, skills, and bonuses. Players can purchase packages of abilities, or they can customize their characters.

\par \tab Demihumans can save 5 character points from this process and apply them at any other point in the character creation process. Humans can save 10 points. Nonstandard races such as lizard men and thri\_kreen cannot save any racial character points
\emdash any points not spent on racial abilities are lost.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Racial Variants
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab Numerous off\_shoot races can be player characters. For example, a player fond of dwarven characters could choose from the hill, mountain, gray, or deep varieties. Players who select such a character pur
chase the standard package of abilities for the subrace. Optionally, a player can customize a character by choosing individual abilities from the list for that race. Generally, buying a package of abilities is the most efficient way to spend character poi
nts.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Racial Languages:}{\fs24 
 A character from a demihuman subrace receives an initial package of languages for no additional cost in character points. However, no character can know more languages than his or her Intelligence/Knowledge score allows (see Table 9)
. If a racial package offers more languages than a character can know, the player can choose which languages the character knows.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Dwarves }{\fs24  Dwarves are a short, stocky race, averaging about 4\endash 41_2' tall. They often have ruddy complexions, dark hair, and pi
ercing, dark eyes. The natural life span for a dwarf is about 350 years. They are a serious lot, given rarely to frivolity. They prefer a good, hard day\rquote s work. Though sometimes considered dour or taciturn, few doubt the dwarves\rquote 
 courage or bravery. They have little talent for magic, but excel at fighting, war craft, and arts such as engineering.
\par \tab Dwarves typically live in hilly or mountainous regions, enjoying the strength of the earth and rocks that make up their underground homes. Dwarves mine the earth for precious metals and gems, and they are particularly fond of gold.
\par \tab Because of their resistance to magic, dwarves have difficulty using enchanted items. All magical items not specially suited to the dwarf\rquote s character class have a 20% (on 1d100 roll) to 
malfunction. A check for malfunction is made every time the dwarf attempts to use the item. If the check is passed, the item works until it is turned off, put away, or its duration expires. If the check fails, the magical qualities of the item cannot be c
a
lled upon, though the item might function normally the next time the dwarf tries to use it. Malfunction checks apply to magical wands, rods, staves, rings, amulets, potions, horns, and jewels. The checks do not apply to dwarven clerics using clerical magi
cal items, and to weapons, shields, armor, gauntlets, and girdles. If a dwarf possesses a }{\i\fs24 cursed}{\fs24  item and it malfunctions, the dwarf will recognize the nature of the item and can safely dispose of it.
\par \tab The standard dwarven subraces are: hill, mountain, deep, and gray. A dwarven character might choose to be a fighter, cleric, or thief. A dwarf also can be multi\_classed as a fighter/cleric or a fighter/thief.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Players who choose dwarves for their characters have 45 character points to spend on racial abilities.}{\fs24  These points can be spent to customize a dwarf from the general skill list below, or they can be used to purchase a subrace
\rquote s skill package. Only 5 points can be retained for use later in the character creation process.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Deep Dwarves
\par }{\b\fs24 Character point cost: 45
\par }{\fs24 As their name implies, deep dwarves live farther underground than their hill or mountain relatives. They prefer the security and the isolation that living far beneath the earth provides.
\par \tab The typical deep dwarf is 4\endash 41_2' tall and weighs 120 pounds. They are large\_boned, but thinner than their cousins who live closer to the surface. And they can move easily through the narrow tunnels they dig in the bedrock.
\par \tab Deep dwarves seldom have contact with creatures from the surface\emdash few such creatures can reach remote underground places\emdash 
and the deep dwarves feel no need to dig tunnels to the surface. Most deep dwarves are neutral, lawful neutral, or chaotic neutral. Players may choose any alignment for their deep dwarf characters.
\par \tab }{\fs24\cf6 Languages:}{\fs24  Deep dwarves can speak their own racial dialect, gray dwarf, illithid, troglodyte, deep gnome, and undercommon (the trade language of all Underdark races).
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Deep Dwarves\rquote  Special Abilities
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 Infravision, 90'\tab Saving Throw Bonuses\tab 
\par Melee Combat Bonuses*\tab Mining Detection Abilities
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par * Attack bonus applies vs. drow, troglodytes, and orcs. 
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Deep Dwarf Racial Penalties
\par }{\fs24 \tab Deep dwarves suffer a \endash 1 penalty to all rolls when in bright sunlight or within the radius of a}{\i\fs24  continual light}{\fs24  spell. }{\i\fs24 Light}{\fs24  spells and all other light sources have no effect on a deep dwarf.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Gray Dwarves
\par }{\b\fs24 Character point cost: 45
\par }{\fs24 Also known as duergar, gray dwarves live far underground\emdash at times even beyond the deep dwarves. However, since they occasionally reside near each other, gray and deep dwarves tend to come into conflict over the resources of the Underdark.

\par \tab An average gray dwarf is about 4' tall and weighs 120 pounds. The thinnest of the subraces, gray dwarves almost appear emaciated. Most are bald, and those who are not usually shave their heads. Their bear
ds are white. Gray dwarves rarely venture above ground, as they find sunlight to be painful to their eyes.
\par \tab Most gray dwarves are lawful evil in alignment, with neutral tendencies. Player character gray dwarves can be of any alignment.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Languages:}{\fs24  Duergar begin play with knowledge of their own tongue, deep dwarf, dark elf, illithid, kuo\_toa, troglodyte, and undercommon.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Gray Dwarves\rquote  Special Abilities
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 Infravision, 120'\tab Saving Throw Bonuses\tab 
\par Melee Combat Bonuses*\tab Stealth
\par Mining Detection Abilities
\par 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 * No attack bonus applies , the character receives only the defensive benefits. 
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Gray Dwarf Racial Penalties
\par }{\fs24 \tab Gray dwarves suffer a \endash 1 penalty on all rolls when exposed to bright sunlight or }{\i\fs24 continual light}{\fs24  spells. Other light sources do not impair them. 
Other types of dwarves distrust duergar, and as such, duergar suffer an initial \endash 2 penalty to reaction rolls from their cousins.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Hill Dwarves
\par }{\b\fs24 Character point cost: 40
\par }{\fs24 Hill dwarves live in areas of rolling headlands\emdash often dwelling underground, though they are known to have outposts on the surface.
\par \tab A typical hill dwarf stands 4' tall and weighs about 150 pounds. He is well\_muscled and stocky, with tan or brown skin, dark hair, and bright eyes. Hill dwarves prefer eart
h tones and seldom wear jewelry. They are the most common of all the dwarven varieties, as they adapt well to life above and below ground. Most hill dwarves are lawful good, but player characters can be of any alignment.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Languages:}{\fs24  Hill dwarves can speak their own tongue as well as other dwarven dialects. In addition, hill dwarves can speak gnome, goblin, orc, and gnoll.
