\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 \tab Bow bonus (5):}{\fs24  A ranger with this ability gains a +1 attack bonus when using any type of bow.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Climbing (10):}{\fs24  A ranger can climb trees, cliffs, and other natural formations. The ranger\rquote 
s climbing score is equal to his Dexterity/Balance score plus the hide in shadows percentage (see Table 22) for a character of his level. Note that a ranger does not have to have t
he hide in shadows ability to have a climbing ability, the two skill simply use similar numbers.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Detect noise (10):}{\fs24  As the thief ability. Use the ranger\rquote s Intuition score plus his move silently percentage to determine his chance for success. 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Empathy with animals (10):}{\fs24  When dealing with a domestic or non\_
hostile animal, the ranger can approach and befriend it automatically. The ranger also can discern the health and nature of such animals. When approaching wild animals or those trained to attack, the anim
al must make a saving throw vs. rods to resist the ranger\rquote s friendly overtures. There is a \endash 1 penalty to the roll for every three experience levels of the ranger. For example, if the approaching ranger is 7th level, the animal\rquote 
s saving throw penalty is \endash 2. If the animal fails the save, the ranger can adjust the animal\rquote s reaction by one category.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Find and remove wilderness traps (10):}{\fs24  These traps include pits, snares, etc. A ranger\rquote s chance for success is equal to his move silently percentage. See the table farther below.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Followers (10):}{\fs24  At 10th level, the ranger attracts 2d6 followers. Use Table 19 in the Player\rquote s Handbook to determine what kind of creatures the ranger attracts. The ranger does not need to build a stronghold or fort.

\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hide in Shadows (5): }{\fs24 A ranger can hide in shadows, as the thief ability, in natural surroundings if he is wearing studded leather or lighter armor. See Table 22 for the ranger\rquote 
s chance of success. The ranger also can attempt to use this ability in other settings, but his chance of success is halved
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Move silently (5):}{\fs24  A ranger can move silently, as the thief ability, in natural surroundings if he is wearing studded leather or lighter armor. See Table 22 for the ranger\rquote 
s chance of success. The ranger also can attempt to use this ability in other settings, but his chance of success is halved.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Pass Without Trace (10):}{\fs24  By selecting this ability at character creation, a ranger gains the druid ability to }{\i\fs24 pass without trace}{\fs24  once a day.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Priest spells (10):}{\fs24  Starting at level eight, a r
anger can learn priest spells of the plant and animal spheres. He follows the normal rules for priest spells, though he does not gain extra spells for a high Wisdom/Intuition score. A ranger cannot use clerical scrolls. The ranger\rquote 
s spell progression is presented on Table 21.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Sneak attack (10):}{\fs24  If a ranger successfully moves silently and hides in shadows, he can sneak\_attack in natural settings, as the thief\rquote 
s backstab ability. The ranger strikes and does backstab damage as a thief of the same level.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Speak with animals (5):}{\fs24  Once a day a ranger can }{\i\fs24 speak with animals}{\fs24 , as the spell.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Special enemy (10):}{\fs24  Rangers focus their efforts on one exceedingly bothersome type of creature. A ranger must choose his special enemy before reaching 2nd level. Sample enem
ies include orcs, trolls, bugbears, and lizard men. The DM must approve the player\rquote 
s choice. From that point on, the ranger gains a +4 bonus to his attack rolls when encountering that type of creature. The ranger can attempt to hide the enmity he feels for these creatures, but he suffers a \endash 
4 penalty on reaction rolls vs. that type of creature. Further, the ranger should seek out such creatures over other foes in combat, unless there is some greater danger.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Tracking proficiency (5):}{\fs24  The ranger gains the tracking proficiency, and the character\rquote 
s tracking skill automatically improves by +1 for every three levels. For example, a 10th level ranger improves his tracking score by +3. 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Two\_weapon style (5):}{\fs24  A ranger can fight with two weapons and suffer no penalt
ies to his attacks rolls. No shield can be used when a ranger fights in this manner. If the ranger wears armor heavier than studded leather, the standard penalties fro two\_weapon fighting apply.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon specialization (10):}{\fs24  This ranger can specialize in a particular weapon. The character point cost must be met in addition.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Optional Restrictions
\par }{\fs24 A ranger can gain bonus character points to spend on the above abilities by accepting voluntary restrictions on his warrior abilities. The restrictions are the same as those for Fighters.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Rogues  }{\fs24 Rogues are scoundrels, living by their wits day to day\emdash often at the expense of others. Not all rogues are outright criminals, but many of them possess a shady past they\rquote 
d rather not have made public. Rogues have several special abilities, the success of which is determined by using percentile dice. Refer to the rogue section of the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24 
 for more information. There are two classes of rogues\emdash thieves and bards. Both use the following table to dictate their level a
dvancements. All rogues roll 1d6 for their hit points from 1st through 10th level. After that, rogues add 2 hit points per level.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Thief
