\par \tab AD&D game bards are loosely derived from Celtic poets who sang their tribes\rquote  histories. Examples include the Pied Piper of Hamelin, Alan\_a\_Dale, Will Scarlet, and Homer. A bard should be glib of tongue, light of heart, and fleet of foot.

\par \tab A bard is a jack\_of\_all\_trades, but a master of none. He fights as rogue, but he can use any weapon. A bard can wear any armor up to, and including, chain mail, but he cannot use a shield.
\par \tab All bards are skilled singers, vocalists, and musicians. A bard character automatically begins play with the proficiencies of Singing and Musical Instrument, in the instrument of the player\rquote 
s choice. The character does not have to expend character points for these bonus proficiencies, but he may choose to use CPs to increase his level of skill by raising his proficiency scores or acquiring the appropriate traits (see Chapter Six).
