\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Alter moods (5):}{\fs24  When performing before a non\_hostile group, the bard can alter the group\rquote 
s mood. This can be achieved via an inspiring poem, a tragic tale, a collection of jokes, or a lively tune. Everyone hearing the bard\rquote s performance must make a saving throw vs. paral
yzation. For every three experience levels the bard possesses apply a \endash 1 modifier to each roll. If the save fails, the group\rquote s current attitude can be shifted one level in the direction chosen by the bard. See the reactions section of the }{
\i\fs24 Dungeon Master Guide}{\fs24  for more details.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Animal friendship (10):}{\fs24  Once a day, the bard can cast the equivalent of an }{\i\fs24 animal friendship}{\fs24  spell by singing a soothing song.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Charm resistance (5): }{\fs24 Bards with this ability gain a +1 bonus to all saving throws versus charm\_like spells and effects. 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Climb walls* (5):}{\fs24  This skill lets bards climb smooth or vertical surfaces.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Counter effects (10):}{\fs24  Bards are able to counter the effects of songs and music used as magical attacks by performing a counter song. All characters within 30' of the bard are immune to such attacks. For example, the bard\rquote 
s adventuring party would be immune to the effects of a harpy\rquote s song. The bard can perform no actions other than a slow walk while countering magic with his music. If he is struck in combat or fail
s any saving throw, the effect of his counter song ends. More details of this power can be found in the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook.
\par }{\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Detect magic* (10):}{\fs24  Bards can spot magical radiations within their line of sight, up to 60 feet away. They can determine the intensity of the magic\emdash dim, faint, moderate, strong, and overwhelming.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Detect noise* (5):}{\fs24  This is the ability to hear sounds others usually can\rquote t.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 History (10):}{\fs24  In his travels, a bard learns a great many things. The bard can read and write his native language and knows his area\rquote 
s local history. In addition, a bard has a 5% chance per level to identify the general purpose, function, and history of any magical item he comes across. The bard need not touch the item, but he must examine it closely.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Pick pockets* (10):}{\fs24 
 A bard uses this skill to pilfer small items from the pouches, pockets, belts, sleeves, packs, etc. of others. A failed attempt means the bard did not come away with an item, but it does not indicate the bard was caught in the act. To determine if a bard
\rquote s attempt was noticed, subtract three times the victim\rquote s experience level from 100. If the bard\rquote 
s roll was equal to or higher than this number, the attempt was noticed. For example, if the thief tried to pick the pocket of a 7th level wizard and failed\emdash and the thief\rquote s roll was 79 or higher\emdash the thief is noticed. (7x3 = 21. 100
\endash 21 = 79.)
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Rally friends (5):}{\fs24  The bard can inspire his compatriots who are about to enter battle. If the bard knows the nature of the threat they face, the bard can spend three full
 rounds rallying his friends with one of the following effects: a +1 bonus on attack rolls, a +1 bonus to saving throws, or a +2 bonus on Morale rolls during the upcoming battle. The range is 10' per level of the bard, and the duration is one round per le
vel of the bard.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Read languages* (5):}{\fs24  A bard needs every edge he can get, and the ability to read languages could put him ahead of his competitors.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Sound resistance (5):}{\fs24  Bards gain a +2 bonus to saving throws vs. sound\_based magical attacks such as: harpy\rquote s song, dragonne\rquote s roar, sphinx\rquote s roar, the shout spell, yeth hound howl, and sirine song.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Scroll use (5/10):}{\fs24  At 10th level, any bard can use magical spell scrolls. By purchasing this skill as a 10\_point ability, he has a chance to read scrolls at any le
vel. If a bard fails to accurately read the scroll, usually something detrimental occurs, such as the spell backfiring. Consult Table 31 for the bard\rquote s chance to read a scroll.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon specialization (10):}{\fs24  This bard can specialize in a particular weapon. The character point cost must be met in addition.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wizard spells (10):}{\fs24  Bards are dabblers in magi
c, studying it in a rather disorganized fashion. They cannot specialize in a school of magic. They can cast spells once they reach 2nd level, and they begin with one to four spells in their spell books. A bard\rquote s spell\_
casting level is equal to his actual experience level. The bard\rquote s spell progression chart follows. 
\par \tab After a bard\rquote s skills are selected, consult the table below to determine base scores. If the bard has skills from the table, record the appropriate scores. These skills can improve as the bard gains levels.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Skill points:}{\fs24  Next, allocate 20 discretionary points among the bard\rquote s \ldblquote scored\rdblquote 
 skills. If the bard possesses only one of those skills, all 20 points are allotted to it. If the bard did not select any of those skills, the 20 points can be spent on nonweapon proficiencies or saved for use during the game.
\par \tab Each time a bard advances a level, he is awarded 15 discretionary points to divide among those abilities.
\par 
