\par \tab }{\fs24\highlight3 Wizards have 40 character points to spend on skills from the following list. These abilities range from 5 to 15 character points. Any unspent points can be used to acquire nonweapon proficiencies or saved for use during the game.}{
\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Access to schools:}{\fs24 
 It costs 5 points per school to which the wizard has access. For example, if a wizard wanted to have access to six schools, the cost would be 30 character points. Normally, wizards spend 40 points to cast spells from the eight standard schools: abjuratio
n, alteration, conjuration/summoning, enchantment/ charm, divination, illusion/phantasm, invocation/evocation, and necromancy. Note that the spells that make up the optional schools of alchemy, geometry, and elemental magic (from the }{\i\fs24 
Tome of Magic}{\fs24 ) are generally included in the standard schools of magic.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Armored wizard (15):}{\fs24  This wizard can cast spells while wearing the armor of his choice.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Automatic spells (5):}{\fs24  When a wizard with this spell gains access to a new level of spells, the character automatically gains a spell of that level for the character\rquote s spellbook. 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Casting reduction (5):}{\fs24  The casting time of all the mage\rquote s spells is reduced by 1. If a spell\rquote s original casting time is 1, it is not reduced.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Combat bonus (10):}{\fs24  A wizard with this ability uses the rogue THAC0 chart.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Detect magic (10): }{\fs24 Characters purchasing this ability can }{\i\fs24 detect magic}{\fs24  once a day for every two levels. For example, a 7th level mage can }{\i\fs24 detect magic}{\fs24 
 three times a day. This is in addition to any }{\i\fs24 detect magic}{\fs24  spells the mage might carry. The character can spot magical radiations within his line of sight, up to 60' away. He can determine the intensity of the magic\emdash 
dim, faint, moderate, strong, and overwhelming.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Extend duration (10): }{\fs24 The duration of all non\_instantaneous spells incr
eases by 1 round for every two experience levels of the wizard. For example, if a 6th level wizard casts a spectral hand spell, it will last 15 rounds rather than 12 rounds.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hit point bonus (10):}{\fs24  These wizards use 1d6 for determining hit points rather than 1d4.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Warrior hit point bonus (5):}{\fs24 
 A wizard with this ability can enjoy the benefits of a high Constitution score as if he were a fighter. For example, if this wizard had a Constitution score of 18, he would gain 4 bonus hit points a level, rather than 2.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Priestly wizards (15):}{\fs24 
 These wizards gain access to one sphere of priest spells and can cast them as if they were wizard spells. These characters must continue to observe the number of various level spells they can cast each day.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Read magic (5):}{\fs24  Characters purchasing this ability can }{\i\fs24 read magic}{\fs24  once a day for every two levels. For example, a 4th level mage can }{\i\fs24 read magic}{\fs24  twice a day. This is in addition to any }{
\i\fs24 read magic}{\fs24  spells the mage might carry.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Resistance to sleep and charm (5):}{\fs24  This wizard gains a +1 bonus to all saving throws versus sleep and charm spells, if the spell or effect allows a saving throw.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon specialization (15):}{\fs24  This wizard can specialize in a particular weapon. The character point cost for gaining proficiency and then specialization must be met in addition to this cost.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Optional Restriction
\par }{\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Limited magical item use (5+):}{\fs24  A wizard with this restriction disdains enchanted items as a crutch for the weak and refuses to use certain categories of magical items. For each categ
ory that is barred to him, he gains 5 CPs . The categories are: potions, oils, and scrolls; rings, rods, staves, and wands, and miscellaneous magical items; and all weapons and armor.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Specialist Wizards
\par }{\fs24 \tab Specialist wizards, such as illusionists, are those
 who choose to concentrate their studies on one particular magic school. The 12 schools of magic are: Abjuration, Alchemy, Alteration, Conjuration/Summoning, Divination, Enchantment/Charm, Geometry, Illusion, Invocation/Evocation, Necromancy, Shadow and S
ong. (In addition, the }{\i\fs24 Tome of Magic}{\fs24 
 describes elemental wizards and wild mages.) For each school of magic, there is an opposition school or schools. Specialist wizards can never cast any spells from as opposing school. The chart that details all the requir
ements, opposition schools, races eligible for each specialization, and minimum ability scores follows.
