\par }{\fs28\cf12 Multi-Classed and Dual Classed Characters}{\fs24   All the rules from the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24  and the }{\i\fs24 Dungeon Master Guide}{\fs24  still apply to multi\_classed and dual\_classed characters. Howe
ver, character points can enhance these divided heroes.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Multi\_classed PCs
\par }{\fs24 \tab Multi\_classed characters are demihumans with two or more professions\_fighter/thief, cleric/wizard, thief/wizard/fighter, etc. Such a character gets to spend the full allotment of C
Ps granted by each class. The points from each class must be spent buying abilities from that class\lquote s list only. The character receives, however, only one class\lquote 
s allowance of CPs for purchasing proficiencies; he gets the largest number allowed by his vari
ous classes. He can choose proficiencies from any of those allowed to any of his classes. When character points are awarded for advancing a level, they must be divided by the number of classes the character has (retain fractions, don\lquote 
t round them up or down).
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Dual\_classed PCs
\par }{\fs24 \tab Dual\_classed characters are humans who change their current classes and adopt new professions. For example, a 5th level fighter might put away his sword and become a 1st level wizard. When he\emdash and any other character\emdash 
changes professions, any unspent character points are lost. The 1st level wizard spends his new character point allotment normally, as if he were adventuring from scratch. When the wizard\rquote 
s new level exceeds that of his first class, he can freely use the abilities of both classes\emdash following all the normal game rules for such a character.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Non-Adventurers}{\fs24   }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option}{\fs24  rules gives DMs the opportunity to expand nonplayer characters. No longer will every blacksmith, jeweler, or horse\_trainer have a THAC0 of 20 and be limited
 to 1d6 hit points. A DM\rquote s cast of regularly\_appearing NPCs can have more substance.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 NPC Tiers
\par }{\fs24 \tab Important NPCs should be created using the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option}{\fs24  rules. This includes generating the six ability scores, determining the 12 subability scores, and de
ciding the race. Character points should be allotted for weapon and nonweapon proficiencies. NPCs who are not adventurers should not be awarded class\_
based character points. Instead, the DM should determine how experienced the NPCs are. Three levels of expertise are defined below.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Apprentice:}{\fs24 
 These NPCs likely are young people who have not yet mastered their trade. Roll 1d6+2 to determine their hit points (plus or minus any Constitution modifiers that apply). Give these NPCs 10 character points to purchase
 proficiencies, traits, and disadvantages. Any character points unspent from the purchase of racial abilities also may spent in this way.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Skilled:}{\fs24  These NPCs tend to be young to middle\_aged adults. Roll 1d8+4 to determine their hit points and add any Cons
titution bonus. Skilled NPCs also 15 character points to spend, plus any unspent from the purchase of racial abilities.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Master:}{\fs24  NPCs such as these have been at their profession a number of years. Masters have 20 character points, plus any unspent from the
 purchase of racial abilities. Master NPCs can be quite hardy. Roll 2d6+6 to determine their hit points. Add any Constitution bonus.
\par \tab If DMs advance an important NPC from one level to the next, simply supply the NPC with an additional 5 character points and add 2 to 6 to the NPC\rquote s hit point total. DMs can expand this system if they wish, creating even more experienced NPCs.

\par 
\par 
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Chapter 5:
\par Character Kits
\par }{\fs24 
\par Many AD&D game players are familiar with the character kits in the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24  Rules Supplement series. }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option}{\fs24 
 kits are different. They are packages of descriptions, proficiencies, benefits, and hindrances that help flesh out characters. In short, the kits give adventurers background and depth.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option}{\fs24  kits are available to more characters than previouly published kits are. No longer are fighters and thieves the only ones who can become swashbucklers. And rogues aren\rquote 
t the only characters who can excel as acrobats. With some exceptions, a character of any class can choose any kit. Many }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option}{\fs24  kits are linked to the social ranks system presented below.
\par \tab Selecting a character kit is free\emdash it has no character point cost. However, it carries a character point benefit. A character with a kit can purchase any of th
e recommended weapon and nonweapon proficiencies listed for 1 character point less than the cost detailed in the proficiency section.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 The Social Ranks System}{\fs24   Rogues, fighters, wizards, and priests can come from any background\emdash from being the 10th son in a peasant family to the first\_born of a noble house. As such, they can be of any social rank.
\par \tab If a campaign is set in a large kingdom\rquote s capital, a character with the noble kit could be a member of one of the nation\rquote s rich, powerful families\emdash perhaps even th
e favored offspring of the rulers. His relatives, however, might not be too happy that he spends so much time knee\_deep in the filthy muck of dungeons, hacking atrocious beasts to bits for a living. That is hardly a suitable \ldblquote noble\rdblquote 
 image. Another character might have the barbarian kit, and the player must decide how his rough and rugged fur\_clad character came to be in a large city at the time the campaign starts.
\par \tab A character\rquote s initial social rank should not limit his advancement in society. A peasant her
o might always feel uncomfortable when standing before his king. But if his heroic deeds warrant such an appearance, he should be granted the opportunity. The only limit on how high a character can rise in society\emdash and in social rank\emdash 
is set by his actions.
\par \tab Social rank is tied to many character kits. Players can take three approaches to selecting a kit. A player can roll at random to determine his character\rquote 
s kit; simply pick a kit, then roll on the social rank table underneath that kit; or, he can roll on the social rank table below and see what kit possibilities open up for his character.
\par \tab To determine social rank first, roll 2d6 on the chart below. Samples of people who might fit into various classes follow. Some DMs might allow players to choose a rank that fits their character concept.
