\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  The animal master mu
st protect his companion, not send it into dangerous areas to spring hidden traps. Should an animal master lose his companion through carelessness or by capriciously placing the animal in danger, he loses 10% of his current experience point total, and he 
l
oses his affinity to that species. No other creature of that type will serve as a companion for that animal master. No penalties are assessed if the animal dies through natural causes, accidentally, or while following orders of a reasonable nature. (Howev
er, it\rquote s reasonable for animals with combat skills to defend their masters.) 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Standard as per the character\rquote s class.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Assassin}{\fs24   This kit is intended for evil aligned characters, as the act of killing for pay cann
ot be justified as a good act. In any society there exists an element that wishes to dispose of rivals, business competitors, and any others deemed to be in the way. It is to this element that the assassin, a hired killer, caters. Unlike simple warriors o
r soldiers, the assassin relies on anonymity and surprise to earn his living. A successful assassin may not even be seen by his target. Slipping out of the darkness or a crowd of friends, the assassin does his dark deed and disappears.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Assassins tend to be well\_off, as their services are expensive. Further, they often are educated, which helps them fit into different segments of society to stalk their prey. Roll 2d6 to determine an assassin
\rquote s rank.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1620\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 3\tab Lower Class
\par \tab 4\endash 6\tab Lower Middle Class
\par \tab 7\endash 11\tab Upper Middle Class
\par \tab 12\tab Upper Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24  To take this kit, a character must have a minimum Intelligence/Reason of 10 and a minimum Wisdom/Willpower of 12. The assassin kit is barred to paladins, rangers, and druids. This kit is 
open to all player character races. The character must have evil alignment.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  An assassin can use any weapon allowed to his class. However, many assassins have one weapon with which they prefer to perform their acts of murder. As he gr
ows more notorious, evidence of a particular weapon becomes the assassin\rquote s calling card.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Etiquette, riding (any), healing, disguise, forgery, modern languages, tracking, reading lips. If the DM allows poison, the assas
sin must have the herbalism proficiency to use various toxins. Herbalism gives the character the knowledge to use natural plants and herbs to produce a variety of poultices, salves, and toxic compounds. Poison strengths and rules are listed in the }{
\i\fs24 Dungeon Master Guide.
\par }{\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  An assassin must be able to move quickly and quietly, and travel as lightly as possible. Light armor and small, easily concealed weapons are common accouterments.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits:}{\fs24  Alertness, ambidexterity, glibness, precise memory.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  The assassin\rquote 
s knowledge of poisons allows him a 5% chance per level to identify toxic substances. If the assassin has the herbalism proficiency, a +10% bonus is added to the final number. The means of identifying poisons include sight, smell, taste, or by a victim
\rquote s symptoms. An assassin with the healing proficiency can treat poison victims with a +1 bonus to his proficiency check.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  Assassins suffer a \endash 4 reaction roll penalty whenever they encounter someone who knows their profession.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Assassins receive 150% of the amount listed for their chosen class.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Barbarian}{\fs24   The barbarian is perhaps the most common fantasy\_fiction archetype. Powerfully built, immensely strong, and clad in furs and skins, the barbarian often is seen as a muscle\_
bound bully. However, barbarians are more than that. They arrive in the campaign\rquote s setting from some distant, primitive land where people must still fight the environment at every turn just to survive. Playing a barbarian is all about working 
the mystique of a primitive culture into a more \ldblquote civilized\rdblquote  world.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Barbarians might be wealthy in their own villages, but they won\rquote t be high on the social or economical ladder of the civilized community they are now a part of. Roll 2d6 to determine the barbarian\rquote 
s rank.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1620\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 8\tab Lower Class
\par \tab 9\endash 12\tab Lower Middle Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24  Barbarians must have minimum Strength/Stamina and Constitution/Health scores of 13. The barbarian kit is barred to paladins, clerics, and bards. This kit is open to all player character races.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Barbarians prefer simple, large, and dependable weapons. Suggested weapons include: axe (all), sword (all), club, dagger, knife, war hammer, mace, sling, spear, javelin, long bow, and qua
rterstaff. The following weapons are likely inappropriate for barbarians\emdash at least until they are immersed in a new culture\emdash crossbow (all), polearms (all), flail (all), lances (all).
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Endurance, animal handling, animal training, bowyer/fletcher, fire\_building, fishing, riding (land\_based), hunting, mountaineering, running, survival, tracking.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  A barbarian cannot initially purchase armor heavier than splint mail, banded mail, or bronze plate mail. Of course, onc
e exposed to superior forms of armor in the campaign, the barbarian may choose to purchase other armor types.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits:}{\fs24  Alertness, direction sense, immunities, keen senses, light sleeper, weather sense.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits/Hindrances:}{\fs24  Barbarians are intense, and NPCs tend to have very strong reactions to them. People either are drawn by the barbarian\rquote 
s animal magnetism or repulsed by his primitive qualities. This effect comes into play when NPCs meet barbarians for the first time. If the NPC\rquote s reaction roll result is 8 or less, an additional \endash 
2 bonus is applied to the result. For example, if the character is acting indifferently toward a shopkeeper and the shopkeeper\rquote s reaction result is a 7, the shopkeeper is }{\i\fs24 indifferent}{\fs24 . However, since the character is a 
barbarian, the \endash 2 bonus applies, lowering the shopkeeper\rquote s result to a 5\emdash a }{\i\fs24 friendly}{\fs24  response. The shopkeeper has been won over by the barbarian\rquote s presence. However, if the shopkeeper\rquote 
s reaction roll was a 14 or higher, the modifier becomes a +2 penalty, resulting in a 16\emdash }{\i\fs24 threatening}{\fs24 \emdash score. For more information on reaction results, see the }{\i\fs24 Dungeon Master Guide.
\par }{\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  A barbarian begins with the standard amount of money for his class, but all of it must be spent before play begins. The DM might allow the B
arbarian to retain a few silver pieces or a handful of coppers, but not much more.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Beggars}{\fs24   Beggars earn a living off the generosity of others. They are a raggedy lot who make others pity them or who deceive others into giving them money. A beggar might d
ress in rags and huddle on a street corner, calling out to strangers for coppers so he can buy food. Perhaps he pretends to be maimed, old, or senile, and begs coins from those more fortunate. Some beggars might actually be down on their luck and in need 
o
f a generous handout. But most of them are capable of working. A few have begging perfected to a fine art. These are swindlers who create elaborate schemes to separate the wealthy from their money. Swindlers might coerce businessmen to invest in a nonexis
t
ent company, con lords and ladies into betting on imaginary horse races, and romance people into marriages that leaves the marks copperless and abandoned. Beggars adventure to get out of town if city guards are giving them too much trouble or if they want
 to score treasure 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Beggars usually occupy the lower tier of society. Roll 2d6 to determine his social rank at the beginning of the campaign.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1620\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Social rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 7\tab Lower Class
\par \tab 8\endash 12\tab Lower Middle Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24  Beggars must be cha
otic in alignment and have a Charisma/Leadership score of at least 10. This kit is barred to all optional races except kobolds, goblins, and mongrelmen, as well as paladins, rangers, druids, and wizards of all types.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Beggars prefer s
mall weapons that can be concealed. That way they look defenseless. These weapons include daggers, knives, saps, and slings. However, when adventuring they use any weapons allowed their character class.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Disguise, forgery, gaming, ventriloquism, seamstress/tailor.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  While begging, these characters dress in rags or whatever other clothing is appropriate to their scam. However, when adventuring they don the best armor and weapons they own.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits: }{\fs24 Glibness, impersonation, inherent immunity to cold, inherent immunity to heat.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  Beggars receive one free nonweapon proficiency at the time of character creation\emdash if the proficiency comes from the above recommended list.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  These characters are not accepted into upper middle class and upper class social circles\emdash at least in the community in which the beggar is plying his trade. This equates to a \endash 
4 reaction roll penalty if the beggar is trying to socialize with the upper crust.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Beggars begin with the normal starting gold for their character class.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Cavalier}{\fs24   This kit is appropriate only for good\_aligned characters, as cavaliers are of brave heart and honorable bearing. They are the devoted heroes of the realm. Warriors, wizards, and c
lerics who love their country, their sovereign, and their people can be cavaliers. Cavaliers are the noblest of nobles, but they do not use their positions in society simply to further their own ends. They strive to aid the less fortunate, protect their r
uler, and save their country\emdash whenever the need arises. Cavaliers can be independent, or they might be employed as agents of the state, perhaps taking orders from the king or queen.
\par \tab Social ranks: Though anyone with the right intentions and determination can be a cavalier, it is more likely these individuals come from the higher echelons of a community. Roll 2d6 to determine a cavalier\rquote s rank.
\par 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\tab Lower Class
\par \tab 3\tab Lower Middle Class
\par \tab 4\endash 7\tab Upper Middle Class
\par \tab 8\endash 12\tab Upper Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24  Cavaliers must have a minimum score of 13 in their class\rquote s prime requisite. Also, all cavaliers must a minimum Charisma/Leadership of 14. Cavaliers can be humans, elves, half\_elves, or dw
arves; this kit is barred to rangers, thieves, druids, and bards.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Cavaliers can choose any weapons allowable by their class. Swords and lances are especially appropriate for warrior cavaliers, as they are suitably noble weapons for cavaliers to carry.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Etiquette, riding (any), heraldry, history (local or ancient), languages (modern or ancient), animal handling, dancing, reading/writing, artistic ability, and gaming.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  Cavaliers can equip themselves how they choose. However, a cavalier must purchase a mount as soon as he can afford one. Not only does a handsome mount increase the cavalier\rquote 
s noble bearing, one will likely come in very handy on his many travels.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits:}{\fs24  Allure, artistic ability, glibness, lucky, music/singing, or music/instrument.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  So good\_hearted and strong\_willed is the cavalier that he gains a +2 saving throw bonus against all mind\_affecting magics. Such magics include, but are not necessarily limited to: }{\i\fs24 
charm person, friends, hypnotism, sleep, irritation, ray of enfeeblement, fear, confusion, geas, command, enthrall, cloak of bravery, }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 symbol.}{\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  Most cavaliers have a strong sense of honor, meaning they will only fight fairly and chivalrously. For example, during a combat on a high cliff\_top, the cavalier\rquote 
s foe might slip over the edge and manage to catch onto a handhold. The cavalier could not leave the miscreant to that fate, nor would he loosen his foe\rquote s grip on th
e cliff. Rather, the cavalier would help the foe up, and then continue the combat. Neither would a cavalier kill any enemy no longer capable of self defense or one who had surrendered in good faith. Because of this code, a cavalier can be difficult to pla
y properly.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Unless the cavalier comes from the lower class, he begins with 150% of the starting cash for his profession. If he comes from the lower class, he begins with the normal starting cash.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Diplomat}{\fs24   In any campaign that involves government and politics, diplomats and other courtiers are bound to appear. Diplomats can bring important messages to neighboring countries. They might\emdash as adventurers\emdash 
explore uncharted regions and act as emissaries. Or they could be attached to a foreign court as their lord\rquote s representative, advising the foreign ruler of the attitudes and policies of the diplomat\rquote 
s homeland. Diplomats also act as their rulers\rquote  eyes and ears in a distant court.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Diplomats are groomed from the higher stations in society. Roll 2d6 to determine their rank.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 6\tab Upper Middle Class
\par \tab 7\endash 12\tab Upper Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24  A diplomat must have a minimum Intelligence/Knowledge score of 10 and minimum Wisdom/Intuition and Charisma/Appearance scores of 12. The diplomat kit is barred to half\_orcs, half\_
ogres, and any of the optional races except centaurs, githzerai, satyrs, and swanmays.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24 
 A diplomat can wield any weapons that fit his character class. However, since a diplomat is usually a peaceful individual, if he wears heavy armor and carries lots of weapons he might be viewed suspiciously. Diplomats are 
at their best appearing inoffensive.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Dancing, etiquette, heraldry, history (any), languages (any, numerous preferred), reading lips, and riding (any).
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  A diplomat can equip himself however he desires. However, his possessions should be of superior quality and appearance.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits:}{\fs24  Allure, alertness, artistic ability, glibness, music (any), obscure knowledge, and precise memory.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  Since the diplomat\rquote s nature is to put others at ease, he receives a +2 bonus to all reaction rolls.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  Diplomats are often far from home, essentially at the mercy of a foreign ruler. If the diplomat delivers a message that the ruler doesn\rquote t appreciate, the ruler may take out his anger on the messenger. Du
ring wartime, diplomats live especially dangerous lives, as they frequently must travel across enemy lines and battlefields to perform their duties.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Diplomats begin with double the amount of gold allotted to their class.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Explorers}{\fs24   Explorers live to see what\rquote 
s over the next mountain or to sail beyond the countries at the edge of a map. They yearn to discover new places, people, and wonders. And their wanderlust drives them ever on. They make perfect adventurers, never staying in one town for more 
than a few days or weeks. And they are always eager to explore ruins, abandoned temples, and cave complexes. Many explorers are demihumans who, not fitting into human\_dominated towns, turned to traveling. Others are half\_breeds, such as half\_
elves, half\_orcs, and half\_ogres, who found more acceptance in the wilderness than in either of their parent\rquote s communities. Of course, there are human explorers, too, these leaving their homes simply because they had to know what\rquote 
s out there.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Explorers come from all walks of life. Roll 2d6 to determine social rank at the beginning of the campaign.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Social rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 3\tab Lower Class
\par \tab 4\endash 6\tab Lower Middle Class
\par \tab 7\endash 10\tab Upper Middle Class
\par \tab 11\endash 12\tab Upper Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24  Explorers must have a minimum of 12 fo
r Wisdom/ Willpower and Intelligence/Knowledge scores. This kit is open to all player character races and classes.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  These characters can select any weapon proficiencies allowed their adventuring classes.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Direction sense, fire\_building, fishing, survival, weather sense, seamanship, local history, ancient history, reading/writing.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment: }{\fs24 Explorers are practical and try to carry all of their arms, armor, and belongings with them. They prize extra\_dimensional spaces and magical carts and boxes such as}{\i\fs24 : bags of holding, Heward\rquote 
s handy haversacks, quivers of Ehlonna, portable holes, mouse carts, flatboxes}{\fs24 , and more. 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits:}{\fs24  Internal compass, inherent immunity to cold, inherent immunity to heat, keen eyesight, keen hearing, precise memory.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  Explorers gain the survival nonweapon proficiency at no cost, and they can choose any two terrain types to apply it to. Possible terrains are: arctic, woodland, desert, steppe, mountain, and tropical.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  These characters cannot stay in one place too long, calling no place home unless \ldblquote home\rdblquote 
 is a ship or a covered wagon. Therefore, explorers cannot spend more than a few weeks at best in any given city or keep.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth: }{\fs24 Explorers begin with the maximum amount of gold allowed their character class.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Gladiator }{\fs24  Gladiators are trained to fight people and creatures for others\rquote  entertainment. Most are flashy, outlandish showmen whose battle tactics often cater to the crowd first, their survi
val second. Gladiators can be slaves who are forced to fight, or they can be free men who choose to make a living this way. If this kit is used in a campaign, there must either be a culture that publicly allows this activity, or else the fights might be i
llegal\emdash held in secret places and backed by society\rquote s shady element. Legal or not, the gladiator definitely makes his living in an urban setting. If the campaign doesn\rquote 
t revolve around, or at least contain, a sizable city, this kit could be inappropriate.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Gladiators can come from almost any economic background, though it is more likely they come from the lower rungs of the community. Roll 2d6 to determine the rank of a gladiator.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 6\tab Lower Class
\par \tab 7\endash 10\tab Lower Middle Class
\par \tab 11\endash 12\tab Upper Middle Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24 
 A gladiator must have Strength/Muscle and Constitution/Fitness scores of 13 or greater. This kit is barred to satyrs and swanmays. In addition, paladins, rangers, wizards, thieves, and bards may not choose this kit.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Only melee and hurled missile weapons\emdash no bows, slings, etc.\emdash 
can be used by such a character, as gladiatorial combats are not about firing arrows at one another. A gladiator who kills an opponent without endangering his own life in mortal 
combat is not much of a showman. Possible gladiator weapons include: daggers, swords (all, but short swords are most common), scimitars, spears, tridents, polearms (all), war hammers, maces (all), scourges, nets, and whips.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Blind\_fighting, endurance, gaming, jumping, tumbling, armorer, weaponsmithing, healing, and rope use.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  Gladiators can have whatever weapons fit their classes. However, they are initially limited in armor types. Gladiators cannot begin with anything greater than studded leather.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits:}{\fs24  Fast healer, immunities, keen senses, light sleeper, lucky.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  Gladiators enjoy a +1 bonus on attack rolls with one particular melee weapon. For example, a gladiator could choose a +1 attack bonus with the short sword.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  As gladiators are trained to make combats last a long while and to play to any crowd, they suffer a +1 penalty to their initiative rolls. They take this time to show off, display their weapon prowess, or bait th
eir opponent.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Standard as per the character\rquote s class.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Jester}{\fs24   Comedians, clowns, jokesters, and tricksters\emdash these make up the jester kit, and those individuals who fit this kit live to make others laugh. Jesters strive to perfect their humor\emdash whethe
r it be slapstick, dark comedy, or simple ribald jokes. Some dress in gaudy costumes and adorn themselves with bells and shiny buttons. Others dress like the common man and blend in with the crowd. They spend their nights in kings\rquote 
 courts, at nobles\rquote  parties, or in crowded taverns, and they tailor their humor to their audience. They adventure to gain wealth and friends\emdash 
and to acquire material for their next performances. The most noted jesters are gnomes because of their exaggerated visages.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Jesters are found at every level in society, though the majority of them come from the middle class. Roll 2d6 to determine social rank at the beginning of the campaign.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Social rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\tab Lower Class
\par \tab 3\endash 7\tab Lower Middle Class
\par \tab 8\endash 11\tab Upper Middle Class
\par \tab 12\tab Upper Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24 
 Jesters must have a minimum Intelligence/Reason of 12 and a minimum Charisma/Leadership of 13. Dwarves, elves, and any of the optional PC races except for kobolds or goblins may not choose this kit. Only bards and thieves may choose this kit. 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  These characters can select any weapon proficiencies allowed their adventuring classes.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Singing, etiquette, modern languages, reading/writing, ventriloquism, juggling, disguise, jumping.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  While adventuring, jesters outfit themselves with weapons and armor allowed their character class. However, they are ever on the lookout for unusual clothes, props, noise\_
makers, and accouterments that can be used in their act.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits:}{\fs24  Precise memory, obscure knowledge, lucky, keen hearing, empathy.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits: }{\fs24 Jesters enjoy a +1 bonus to Charisma when they are working an audience.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  Jesters are often not taken seriously. When trying to conduct business deals or when socializing, NPCs have a \endash 1 reaction roll penalty toward them.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Jesters begin with the normal amount of gold allowed their character class.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Mariner}{\fs24   Mariners are devoted to the sea. They may be young, old, poor or wealthy enough to own the ship they sail. They travel the world\rquote 
s oceans because they love it. They live for trading, exploring, and discovering new lands.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Mariners tend to be educated individuals from families with a reasonable amount of gold. Roll 2d6 to determine the rank of a mariner.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 6\tab Lower Middle Class
\par \tab 7\endash 10\tab Upper Middle Class
\par \tab 11\endash 12\tab Upper Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements: }{\fs24 A mariner must have a minimum Intelligence/Knowledge score of 9. The mariner kit is prohibited for dwarves, aarakocra, alaghi, bullywugs, centaurs, mongrelmen, ogres, orcs, satyrs, swanmays, thri\_
kreen, and wemics.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Mariners can be versed in any weapons applicable to their classes. However, weapons such as spears, pole\_arms, and nets are practical.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Weather sense, navigation, fishing, seamanship, swimming, rope use.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  Mariners can carry whatever equipment they desire, however much of it should be useful in their trade. Further, these individuals tend to wear little or no armor, as heavy armor is not practical on a ship. They prize}
{\i\fs24  rings of protection}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 bracers of defense.
\par }{\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits: }{\fs24 Keen senses, light sleeper, lucky.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits: }{\fs24 Mariners enjoy a +2 bonus on reaction rolls from sailors, ship captains, and aquatic races. And they gain a +1 to hit bonus with nets.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  Because mariners are so at home on the sea, they are often uncomfortable on land. When there is no water in sight, they are out of their element, and they suffer a \endash 2 reaction roll penalty.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Standard for the character\rquote s class.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Merchants}{\fs24   Merchants are vital to any civilization, for they buy, sell, or barter the goods and services that a society demands. Merchants include local fish\_
mongers, street vendors, shop keepers, caravan owners, and more. Some have permanent stores established in a thriving business district. Others travel to distant lands to bring back rare treasures. Adven
turers are natural merchants, as they often visit many strange and exotic places and acquire unusual and valuable goods. Merchant adventurers have an outlet to sell many of the material treasures such as tapestries, statuary, gems, and jewelry they often 
accumulate.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Merchants come from many walks of life. Roll 2d6 to determine the rank of a merchant. Optionally, grant that merchant the appropriate business and income.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx3600\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Rank\tab Business
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 3\tab Lower Class\tab Works for someone else
\par \tab 4\endash 7\tab Lower Middle Class\tab Owns a stall or rents a storefront
\par \tab 8\endash 10\tab Upper Middle Class\tab Owns a storefront
\par \tab 11\endash 12\tab Upper Class\tab Owns a large storefront
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24  A merchant must have minimum Intelligence/Knowledge and Charisma/Appearance scores of 9. This kit is clos
ed to paladins, rangers, druids and all charcters of the following races: alaghi, minotaur, mongrelman, ogre, satyr, swanmay, thri\_kreen, and wemic.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Merchants can wield any weapons appropriate to their classes. However, a merchant who wears heavy armor and carries several large weapons might make their customers nervous\emdash 
unless the merchant is in the business of selling arms and armor.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Appraising, carpentry, cobbling, etiquette, reading/writing, modern languages, local history, forgery, tailoring, weaving.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  Merchants can carry whatever equipment they desire, however much of it should be useful in their trade. Many merchants make sure they have plenty of leather sacks and other containers 
to store goods they might acquire. They prize }{\i\fs24 bags of holding}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 flatboxes}{\fs24 .
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits:}{\fs24  Glibness, keen senses, precise memory, alertness, obscure knowledge.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  Because they are often welcomed into communities, merchants receive a +1 reaction roll bonus in city settings. In addition, merchants with the appraising proficiency gain a permanent +2 bonus to that skill.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  While they are initially welcomed for the goods and services they bring, not all merchants are forthright in their
 business dealings. Any merchant who is publicly accused of cheating his customers (whether the accusation is true), loses the reaction bonus above. Further, he suffers a \endash 2 penalty to all reaction rolls until his innocence is proven.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  A merchant begins with the maximum amount of gold allotted to his class.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Mystic }{\fs24  Thoughtful and introspective, the mystic seeks to find the answers to the mysteries of the universe by traveling across the gl
obe. Nonviolent by nature, the inquisitive mystic seeks only truth, and he avoids confrontations when possible. However, if the mystic must battle his way to enlightenment, he will do so. Mystics are usually found among cultures that place a high value on
 art, philosophy, and scholarship. As seekers of knowledge, mystics often become adventurers while they search for the answers they seek.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Mystics tend to be well\_educated, coming from the more affluent segments of society. Roll 2d6 to determine the rank of a mystic.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 7\tab Upper Middle Class
\par \tab 8\endash 12\tab Upper Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24  A mystic must have a minimum Wisdom/Intuition score of 13. This kit is closed to thieves and bards. Only characters of the following races can choose this kit: human, elf, half\_
elf, gnome, halfling, aarakocra, alaghi, centaur, githzerai, swanmay, and wemic.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Mystics can wield any weapons appropriate to their classes. However, most prefer lighter weapons which do not weigh them down.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24 
 Astrology, astronomy, religion, etiquette, languages (modern or ancient), history, (local or ancient), reading/writing, heraldry, riding (any), herbalism, healing, gem cutting, musical instrument, navigation.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  Mystics can select whatever equipment they desire and can afford. However, these characters are known to travel unencumbered, so care should be taken in what they purchase and acquire.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits:}{\fs24  Artistic ability, empathy, obscure knowledge, ancient history, precise memory.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits: }{\fs24 A mystic can temporarily boost one of his 12 subability scores by +2. If the mystic is a ranger with a 16 Balance score, by meditating the score can be temporarily increased to an 18. If the mystic is a warrior trying 
to boost a Muscle score of 18/30, the bonus counts as 20% rather than 2 points\emdash each point equates to 10%. The mystic warrior\rquote 
s Muscle is temporarily increased to 18/50. To gain this subability bonus, the mystic must meditate, building up his body and mind for this feat. The subability score remains boosted for one\_third of the mystic\rquote 
s meditation time. If the Mystic mediated for three uninterrupted hours, he could boost a subability score for one hour.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  The process of meditation requires effort.
 While a mystic could meditate in the cabin of a ship, he could not do so if that ship were in the midst of a terrible storm. Attacks or very loud noises also disrupt meditation. The time spent in meditation does not alleviate the mystic\rquote 
s need for food and sleep. So it is unlikely that a mystic could spend three days in meditation in preparation for an adventure. Further, a mystic cannot gain multiple meditation bonuses at one time.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  A mystic is interested in knowledge, not material items. As such, he cannot own more than he can carry. A mount is an exception, as such a beast is useful in taking the mystic to new places.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Nobles}{\fs24   Nobles are those born to the highest ranks of society. They are rich, dress well, and they usually spend money frivolousl
y. They are the privileged few the common people idolize. And they are the symbols of what others often consider the best in the world.
\par \tab This leads many nobles to believe they are better than everyone around them\emdash not just in wealth or social standing, but 
in manner and abilities. This sense of superiority can manifest itself as overwhelming pride, arrogance, and condescending snobbery. These nobles use their wealth, standing, and power to take what they want from those weaker than themselves. They can be a
s brutal, savage, and unthinking as the horrific monsters they slay.
\par \tab Not all nobles are like this, however. Some feel a certain sense of stewardship for the unwashed hordes. These nobles consider it their duty to do all in their power to better the world\emdash a
s long as their lifestyles are not jeopardized. They can be chivalrous toward members of the opposite sex, and they might occasionally stop to chat with peasants. These nobles mean well, but they may be out of touch with what is important in the lives of 
the common people.
\par \tab Nobles need not be played as brutal tyrants or out\_of\_touch fops and dandies, but there are plenty of wealthy individuals who fit those two archetypes.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Nobles are only found in the upper echelons of society. Roll 2d6 to determine the rank of a noble.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 4\tab Upper Middle Class
\par \tab 5\endash 12\tab Upper Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24  Nobles need only meet the requirements of their adventuring class. This kit is open to all classes and races except mongrelmen.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Any allowed by their adventuring class.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Etiquette, heraldry, riding (any), dancing, languages (ancient or modern), history, (local or ancient), reading/writing, religion, gambling, musical instrument, animal handling,
 hunting, and gaming.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  Regardless of his class, he must purchase a mount and tack, as no self\_
respecting noble would allow himself to be seen on foot when out and about. Further, all armor, weapons, clothes and accouterments must be of the finest quality.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits:}{\fs24  Allure, artistic ability, glibness, lucky, music (any).
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  Because of his superior manner of dress, speech, and decorum, NPC nobles will recognize the player character\rquote s status and accord him the proper respect. Play
er character nobles receive a +2 reaction roll bonus when interacting with individuals from the upper class and upper middle class.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  Because the noble demands the finest things in life, he pays more money for meals, board, equipment, etc. In ad
dition, many vendors recognize the noble as being wealthy and raise their prices accordingly. A noble must add 25% to the price of any item or service he wishes to purchase.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Nobles begin with twice the normal starting gold of their class to reflect their higher level of resources.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Outlaw}{\fs24   These characters spit in the face of authority and scoff at the mention of \ldblquote the law.\rdblquote  They are men and women wanted by the campaign\rquote s government officials and law\_enforcement authorities.
\par \tab Some outlaws are the he
roic Robin Hood type. They become outlaws because they oppose an evil, corrupt, or tyrannical regime. These outlaws are more concerned with justice than the strict letter of the law. And while they willingly break laws, they do it for the common good
\emdash robbing from the rich to give to the poor, smiting the minions of the vile administration, and generally making life as uncomfortable as possible for those who\rquote ve turned the law to their own wicked ends.
\par \tab Other outlaws are bandits and highwaymen who inhabit the fringes of many civilizations hoping to rob innocent passersby of their hard\_earned valuables. These despicable louts truly give the term \ldblquote outlaw\rdblquote  a bad name.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Outlaws typically come from the poorer segments of society. Roll 2d6 to determine an outlaw\rquote s social rank.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Rank}{\fs24 
\par \tab 2\endash 7\tab Lower Class
\par \tab 8\endash 12\tab Lower Middle Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24  As many Outlaws must leave the comforts of civilization behind to avoid capture, they must be hardy souls. All outlaws must have minimum Strength/Stamina an
d Constitution/Health scores of 12. This kit is open to all races and classes, but paladin outlaws require special approval from the DM.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Outlaws can wield whatever weapons are allowed by their class. However, they tend to favor missile weapons because they like to strike at the forces of the law from a safe distance.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Rope use, set snares, survival tracking, fire\_building, riding (any), animal lore, bowyer/fletcher, hunting, running, local history, disguise, tumbling.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  Characters who are on the run, such as outlaws, are apt to be lightly equipped to allow for quick, quiet movements.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits: }{\fs24 Alertness, internal compass, glibness, impersonation, keen senses, light sleeper, lucky.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  Outlaws have a designated hideaway, a place where they can feel safe. Possible locations include a grove in the local forest, a hidden valley, the city\rquote 
s sewer system, or a nearby dungeon. The DM and player should work out the specifics.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  Outlaws always have someone out to get them\emdash usually the collective force of the campaign\rquote s law enforcers. They might have some player characters after
 them, too, if the bounty on their head becomes tempting enough.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Standard for the character\rquote s class.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Peasant Hero}{\fs24   A Peasant hero is the \ldblquote local kid done good.\rdblquote  Whatever his class, whatever his ambitions, this character always remembers that his roots are in the soil of his home\emdash 
be it a bustling village, a quiet hamlet, or a lone farm miles from the nearest neighbors. He might leave his home and become a great general, a mighty wizard, a noble priest, or a master thief\emdash but his home will always be f
oremost in his heart. A peasant hero might fight against tyrannical nobles who are oppressing the common people, or he could simply yearn for the wealth of the nobles\emdash so he can return to his village and build homes, temples, schools, and more.

\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Peasant heroes come from the poorest of families. Roll 2d6 to determine the hero\rquote s social rank.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 9\tab Lower Class
\par \tab 10\endash 12\tab Lower Middle Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24  This kit is open to all classes and races. There are no abilty score requirements.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24 
 Although a peasant hero can wield any weapon allowed by his adventuring class, favored weapons include: dagger, short sword, quarterstaff, spear, short bow, club, hand axe, knife, sickle, sling, and staff sling.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Agriculture, fishing, animal handling, animal lore, blacksmithing, carpentry, cooking, cobbling, fire\_building, pottery, stonemasonry, weaving.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  See \ldblquote Wealth\rdblquote  below.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Traits:}{\fs24  Animal empathy, empathy, immunities, climate sense.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24 
 The disadvantaged often make it through the rough times by helping each other. Therefore, in his homeland, a peasant hero always will be given shelter and any other help his fellows can give. They will hide him and his friends from an evil ruler\rquote 
s troops, heal his wounds, and feed and clothe him. Of course, this is conditional\emdash if the peasant hero has given up his down\_to\_earth ways or mistreats his former friends, he\rquote ll find all doors in the village closed to him.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances: }{\fs24 As he is viewed as s
omething of a hero, a peasant hero will find the people of his homeland coming to him for assistance. If livestock are disappearing from the pens, or the village elder has been jailed for speaking against the nobles, or if another peasant is accused of cr
ime he didn\rquote t commit, the peasant hero will be called upon to help.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Peasant heroes begin with half the starting maximum gold allowed for their adventuring class.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Pirate}{\fs24   Like mariners, pirates love the ocean. However, pirates also love wealth. Evil pirates are the scum of the seas. They\rquote ll attack a ship and kill the crew to a man\emdash 
unless there is a nobleman or wealthy heiress on board they can hold for ransom. These buccaneers have no lofty goals and fight for no cause other than to net the next ship full of booty.
\par \tab Privateers are neutral\_ or good\_aligned pirates who are usually commissioned by a government to prey on the shipping of that government\rquote s enemy. Privateers are oft
en granted Letters of Marque that state they are in the employ of their king or queen. As representatives of a nation, privateers are expected to behave with a code of honor. They accept surrenders, treat prisoners well, and are always deferential to any 
noble captives\emdash especially those of the opposite sex.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Pirates usually come from the middle rungs of the social ladder. Roll 2d6 to determine the rank of a pirate.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 7\tab Lower Middle Class
\par \tab 8\endash 12\tab Upper Middle Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24 
 A life on the sea is rigorous and demanding. Pirates must have minimum Constitution/Health and Dexterity/Balance scores of 12. The pirate kit is prohibited for dwarves, aarakocra, alaghi, bullywugs, centaurs, ogres, orcs, satyrs, swanmays, thri\_
kreen, and wemics. This kit is open to all classes.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Pirates can be versed in any weapons applicable to their classes. However, rapiers and cutlasses are suggested for swashbuckling characters.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Navigation, fishing, seamanship, rope use, gambling, tightrope walking, direction sense, swimming, weather sense.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  Pirates tend to avoid large weapons and heavy armor. Characters who are weighted down have a hard time climbing around in the ship\rquote s rigging a
nd are likely to drown if they are pitched overboard during a storm or a fierce battle.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits:}{\fs24  Alertness, glibness, light sleeper.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  Pirates are accustomed to the pitch and roll of a ship and have an advantage when engaged in melee o
n deck. They gain a +1 bonus to their attack rolls while fighting at sea. Pirates also have a designated hideaway. Such locations include deserted isles, hidden anchorages, a sea\_cave complex beneath the port city, etc.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  Pirates suffer a \endash 2 pen
alty on reaction rolls when they enter a port. If a pirate behaves in a manner appropriate to the setting, this penalty may be reduced and then eliminated upon subsequent visits. Pirates often make a lot of enemies. Hence, pirates are sometimes hunted by 
those they plundered. Pirate characters should never get too complacent\emdash there\rquote s always somebody not too far away who wants the character\rquote s loot or head to show that this sea wolf will prowl the oceans no more.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Standard for the character\rquote s class.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Pugilist}{\fs24 
  Pugilists are characters who prefer to fight with their bare hands. They might make a living by fighting in a ring. Or perhaps they specialize in martial arts. They are strong, tough, and fit into any society that allows professional or street fig
hts. Pugilists make excellent adventurers, as they have quick reflexes and are good in a brawl with monsters. Adventuring gives them an additional outlet for their physical side, and it affords them opportunities to travel to new communities and find new 
fights. Players might want to consult the Combat and Tactics book for information on unarmed combat and martial arts.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Most pugilists come from poorer families in bad parts of town where people learned to fight to get what or simply to defend themselves. Roll 2d6 to determine a pugilist\rquote 
s social rank at the beginning of the campaign.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Social rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 10\tab Lower Class
\par \tab 11\endash 12\tab Lower Middle Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24  Pugilists must have minimum Strength/Muscle and Dexterity/Balance scores of 14. This kit is barred to the following races: aarakocra, alaghi, bullywug, centaur, lizard man, minotaur, swanway, thri\_
kreen, and wemic. The kit is open to all classes except wizards.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24 
 These characters can select any weapon proficiencies allowed their adventuring classes. However, they prefer to fight with their hands. Proficiency and specialization in any form of unarmed combat (punching, wrestling, or martial arts) is recommended for
 the pugilist.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Blind\_fighting, endurance, running.
\par \tab Equipment: Pugilists prefer to fight in loose\_fitting street clothes and unencumbered. They seek magical bracers and rings to make themselves more difficult to be struck. They will wear armor while adventuring\emdash 
if their character class allows it.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits:}{\fs24  Keen eyesight, keen hearing, keen sense of touch, light sleeper, ambidexterity.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  Normally, when a character makes an 
unarmed attack against an armed opponent or a monster with natural attacks, his foe gains an immediate attack of opportunity with a +4 bonus to his attack roll. This makes it very dangerous for unarmed heroes to attack most enemies. However, pugilists are
 skilled at fighting armed opponents and monsters with their bare hands; they are treated as if they were armed when making unarmed attacks.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  Pugilists are admired when they are in a ring and the crowd is cheering them on. However, people from the middle and upper class prefer not to associate with the ruffians. A pugilist\rquote 
s Charisma is effectively lowered by 1 when dealing with those from the middle class and by 2 when speaking to people from the upper class.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Pugilists begin with the normal amount of gold allowed their character class.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Rider}{\fs24   A rider is a character who has developed a bond with his mount. When the character was younger, he became friends with this animal, and the rider and his mount grew up together and grew close. They
 are now all but inseparable. In fact, they share such a bond they can sense each other\rquote s location and general health. Perhaps the rider is an elf who was born in the deep wilderness and who rides into the campaign\rquote 
s city on a giant boar. Maybe the rider is a halfling lass who\emdash with her giant lizard mount\emdash leaves her village behind in pursuit of adventure.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social Ranks:}{\fs24  Riders can come from most walks of life. Roll 2d6 to determine the rider\rquote s social rank.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Rank}{\fs24 
\par \tab 2\endash 4\tab Lower Class
\par \tab 5\endash 10\tab Lower Middle Class
\par \tab 11\endash 12\tab Upper Middle Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24  Riders must have a minimum Charisma/Leadership of 13. This kit is open to the standard player character races, and to flinds, githzerai, goblins, kobolds, orcs, and swanmays. The kit is open to all classes.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Riders can select any weapons that fit with their adventuring professions. Weapons designed to be used while mounted are ideal. These include: lances, bow (any), horseman\rquote s flail, horseman\rquote 
s mace, horseman\rquote s pick, spear, bastard sword, long sword, scimitar, morning star.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies: }{\fs24 Animal lore, animal training, animal handling, riding (any), rope use, charioteering, heraldry, bowyer/fletcher.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  Riders can select any weapons, armor, and other equipment that fits their adventuring class.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits:}{\fs24  Animal empathy, empathy, alertness, keen hearing.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  When this kit is chosen, the player and DM must determine a suitable mount for the character. There are several factors to consider: 1) the climate and terrain of the character\rquote 
s homeland, where he acquired the mount; 2) the character\rquote s race (halflings would have a difficult time riding elephants, while half\_ogres would be too big for a pony) and; 3) the availability of the mount. A rider\rquote 
s mount is similar to a wizard\rquote 
s familiar in that the pair have a bond. Each will know the general state of health of the other, the direction the other is in, and the distance by which they are separated. Depending on its degree of intelligence, the mount
 might be able to understand if its friend is in danger. Many a rider has escaped certain death on the back of a mount that risked its own life to save him.
\par \tab After making a list of possible mounts, the player should select one. DMs might prefer the mount to be selected randomly. If this is the case, use the following method.
\par \tab Roll 1d6. A result of 1\endash 3 indicates the player should roll on the natural creatures table; 4, flying creatures; 5, giant land creatures, and; 6, underwater creatures. Then roll 1d8 on the appropriate mount table.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Table 43: Mounts
