\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances: }{\fs24 If a rider neglects or mistreats his mount, the DM might declare that the mount flees\emdash 
bolts during the night, kicks its way out of a stable, flies away, etc. If this occurs, the rider can never again experience an empathic rapport with a animal. Further, when the rider\rquote 
s mount dies, the rider immediately suffers 2d6 points of damage, due to the emotional loss resulting from the death of a close friend. This damage can be healed. However, if the mount\rquote s death occurred as a result of the rider\rquote 
s negligence, aside from suffering the physical damage, the rider must attempt a saving throw vs. spells. Failure means the rider operates as if he were under a feeblemind spell for the next 2d6 hours.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  A rider begins with the amount of money allowed his adventuring class. He must purchase tack and animal feed with this before using what is left to purchase weapons, armor, and equipment.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Savage}{\fs24   Savages are characters from primitive societies, individuals who lived on their o
wn or in extended families. They differ from barbarians in that they have no established villages or communities. They live off the land by hunting and foraging. They do not herd animals or establish farms. Savages find their way into adventuring groups s
imply by crossing paths with player character heroes. They are quick to become a part of an adventuring family, and they are comfortable traveling and exploring. However, savages are uncomfortable in towns, and they are suspicious of civilized trappings.

\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Since savages do not come from a society, their starting social rank is essentially lower class.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Social rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 11\tab Lower Class
\par \tab 12\tab Lower Middle Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24  Savages must have a minimum Constitution/Fitness score of 13. This kit is open to all races except githzerai and swanmays, and to all classes except paladins.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  At the time of character creation, a savage must choose from the following wooden weapons: quarterstaff, spear, bow, and club. As the chara
cter gains in levels and adventures with others, he can pick up more civilized weapons, such as maces, swords, and daggers.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Animal lore, hunting, running, set snares, survival, tracking, fishing, swimming.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  
Savages begin without any metal equipment. Their weapons are limited to those listed above, and they can wear nothing greater than hide armor. All of their possessions must come from nature, such: as deerskin sacks, buffalo hide blankets, vine rope, woven
 baskets, clay jugs, stone arrowheads, etc.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits:}{\fs24  Keen eyesight, keen hearing, keen sense of touch, keen sense of taste, light sleeper, ambidexterity.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  Savages gain +1 to any three nonweapon proficiencies scores, provided they are selected from the recommended list above.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  Player characters with this kit are uneasy in cities. They suffer a \endash 2 initiative penalty when traveling within the confines of a town or keep.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Savages are allowed to spend up to the normal wea
lth allowed their character class on the weapons, hide armor, and equipment mentioned in this kit. However, whatever is not spent vanishes. Savages begin play with no money.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Scholar}{\fs24   A scholar is driven by his incessant drive for knowledge about a particu
lar topic or family of topics. Adventuring scholars might want to study underground ruins, abandoned temples, mythical beasts, giant insects, haunted woods, or monsters. When not traveling (and taking copious notes at every opportunity), he\rquote 
s likely to be found poring over books, maps, scrolls, and clay tablets. Scholars choose to adventure because they know they can learn more by studying something up close than by reading about it in musty libraries.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Scholars are well\_educated and come from rather affluent families. Roll 2d6 to determine a scholar\rquote s social rank.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 6\tab Upper Middle Class
\par \tab 7\endash 12\tab Upper Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24  A scholar must have a minimum Intelligence/Knowledge of 13. This kit is open to all standard player chacter races and to: centaurs, githzerai, and swanmays. Fighters may not be scholars.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Those that fit with the scholar\rquote s adventuring class.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Reading/writing, history (any), languages (any), heraldry, astrology, astronomy, herbalism, engineering, gem cutting, religion, spellcraft, brewing, etiquette, musical instrument.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  Scholars always must be prepared to record some interesting new bit of knowledge. Therefore, all scholars
 must carry quills, ink, scrolls, and a journal or diary with which to write down any intriguing new facts or theories.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits:}{\fs24  Artistic ability, keen eyesight, keen hearing, precise memory, obscure knowledge.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  Scholars gain a +1 bonus either to Intelligence or Wisdom checks (player\rquote s choice)\emdash including proficiency checks based on Intelligence or Wisdom.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  Since scholars spend so much time reading, theorizing, and studying, they suffer when it comes to physical combat. All scholars suffer a \endash 1 penalty on their initiative rolls when fighting.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Standard for the character\rquote s class.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Scout}{\fs24   Scouts are those characters who are most at home in the wilderness. They can be hunters, loggers, trappers or furriers. Or they might be employed by the campaign\rquote s military forces to patrol the country\rquote 
s borders and keep a furtive eye on the nation\rquote s enemies. As a rule, they are brave\emdash even daring. Few others would voluntarily cross into enemy territory to ascertain troop movements or the locations of the monsters\rquote 
 stronghold. Wilderness is not limited to forests. Any geographic region that is untouched by civilization qualifies. Such areas can include the desert, arctic tundra, tropical rain forests, mountain valleys, or even natural cave complexes\emdash 
perhaps leading to the Underdark.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Scouts come from the lower tiers of society. Roll 2d6 to determine a scout\rquote s social rank.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 5\tab Lower Class
\par \tab 6\endash 12\tab Lower Middle Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24  Scouts must be able to discern a dangerous situation from a lethal one. All scouts need a minimum Wisdom/Intuition score of 12. This kit is open to all races and classes.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Scouts can be proficient in any weapons that fit with their adventuring class. Since they are frequently outnumbered by foes, most scouts select missile weapons to help them even the odds.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Tracking, survival, fire\_building, hunting, mountaineering, rope use, set snares, swimming, carpentry, direction sense, weather sense.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  Scouts do not enter the wilderness unprepared. Scouts must purchase adequate clothing for the season, rations and water, flint and ste
el, rope, bedroll, a small hammer, and pitons. They can purchase any weapons, armor, and other equipment with any gold they have remaining.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits:}{\fs24  Alertness, fast healer, impersonation, keen eyesight, keen hearing, keen sense of touch, light sleeper.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  Scouts gain a +1 bonus to all nonweapon proficiency checks while in the wilderness or natural cave settings.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  Scouts are ill at ease in urban or dungeon settings. They suffer a \endash 1 penalty on all nonweapon proficiency checks when in such locales.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Standard for the character\rquote s class.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Sharpshooter  }{\fs24 Sharpshooters devote their time and effort to becoming extremely proficient with missile weapons. They might be experts with thrown weapons such as the dagger or hand axe. Some make their living as trick\_
shot artists in carnivals and circuses. Sharpshooters may be military specialists assigned to attack and dispose of enemy officers, wizards, airborne foes, or even enemy sharpshooters. Others might be classic archers, able to split an opponent\rquote 
s arrow in a target or knock a foe\rquote s weapon from his hand.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social Ranks:}{\fs24  Sharpshooters usually come from middle\_classed families who have money to spend on weapons and lessons. Roll 2d6 to determine the social rank.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 6\tab Lower Middle Class
\par \tab 7\endash 12\tab Upper Middle Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements: }{\fs24 A sharpshooter must have a minimum Dexterity/Aim of 13. Bugbears, lizard men, minotaurs, ogres, and wemics cannot become sharpsh
ooters. This kit is barred to wizards and priests (although the DM may allow some specialty priests, such as druids, to become sharpshooters).
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  More than half of a sharpshooter\rquote s initial weapon proficiencies (or at least six character
 points) must be allocated on missile weapons. Expenditures for missile weapon specialization or style specializations involving missile weapons count toward the requirements.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Bowyer/fletcher, hunting, heraldry, riding, weaponsmithing.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  A sharpshooter\rquote s first concern is making sure he has enough arrows or other missile weapons. After that, he can purchase whatever armor and equipment is appropriate to his adventuring class.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Traits:}{\fs24  Ambidexterity, keen eyesight, keen hearing, lucky.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  Sharpshooters gain a +1 bonus to attack and damage rolls with one particular missile weapon. The weapon must be chosen at the time of the character\rquote 
s creation. This bonus does not apply to a hurled missile weapon when it is used in 
melee combat. For example, if a sharpshooter threw a spear at an oncoming hobgoblin, he could apply the bonus. If he waited until the hobgoblin closed to melee range, the bonus is no longer applicable.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  Because a sharpshooter concentrates on missile weapons, he suffers a \endash 1 penalty on initiative rolls for melee combat.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Standard for the character\rquote s class.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Smuggler }{\fs24  Characters who earn a living by fencing questionable goods and moving material about behind the backs of government officia
ls are considered smugglers. They are opportunists who have the right connections to guide stolen or suspicious property about from place to place\emdash 
at a considerable profit to themselves. Smugglers rarely steal things themselves. They consider that much more risky than transporting the goods and feigning ignorance about the goods\rquote  origins. And they rarely keep materials they believe stolen
\emdash better to move the objects on to another city. A few smugglers work as fences on the side. Smugglers love the adventurin
g life because it gives them the opportunity to travel and the chance to move goods. A smuggler might jump at the opportunity to delve into a dungeon when the authorities are on the lookout for a matched set of emeralds he has in his belt pouch.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Most smugglers come from the middle class. Roll 2d6 to determine their rank.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Social rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 8\tab Lower Middle Class
\par \tab 9\endash 12\tab Upper Middle Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24  Smugglers must have a minimum Wisdom/Willpower score of 12, as they must have
 enough common sense to help them stay ahead of the law. This kit is open to all races and classes, but paladin smugglers require special approval from the DM.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  These characters can select any weapon proficiencies allowed their adventuring classes.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Appraising, forgery, gem cutting, ancient history, artistic ability, etiquette, pottery, ancient languages, reading/writing.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  Smugglers seek magical bracers and rings to make themselves more difficult to be struck or seen. They will wear armor while adventuring\emdash 
if their character class allows it. However, they prefer to travel in loose\_fitting street clothes and unencumbered.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits:}{\fs24  Keen sense of touch, glibness, impersonation, lucky, obscure knowledge, precise memory.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  If a smuggler chooses the appraising nonweapon proficiency, he gains a +2 bonus to that proficiency score.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  Smugglers on occasion run afoul of the law\emdash or of individuals who try to pass goods via the character. When this happens, a smuggler might have to rely on his fellow adventurers for protection.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth: }{\fs24 Smugglers begin with the maximum amount of gold allotted to their character class to reflect the money they earn from their shady dealings.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Soldier }{\fs24 
 A Soldier is a professional warrior. He might be a field officer, a career sergeant, or a sellsword. Any character class can be a soldier. Warriors and clerics can lead troops into battle; wizards can decimate enemy armies with their powerful, o
ffensive spells; and rogues can infiltrate enemy\_
occupied positions and gain valuable intelligence. If a player is interested in this kit, he should discuss with the DM whether the soldier is a member of a standing military organization or is a mercenary. 
If the character is a member of a military force, his military rank also must be decided.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Soldiers come from all walks of life. However, those from more influential families tend to rise higher in the military career ladder. If the soldier i
s part of an organized unit, roll 2d6 to determine his social and military rank.
\par \tab 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx3600\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Rank\tab Military Title
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 5\tab Lower Class\tab Man\_at\_arms/foot soldier
\par \tab 6\endash 9\tab Lower Middle Class\tab Sergeant/horse soldier
\par \tab 10\endash 11\tab Upper Middle Class\tab Lieutenant
\par }\pard\plain \s1\keep\keepn\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx3600\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\tab 12\tab Upper Class\tab Captain/commander
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20 {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24 
 Soldiers must have a minimum Constitution/Fitness score of 12, as military life is not easy. This kit is open to any player character race except; aarakockra, alaghi. githzerai, minotaur, mongrelman, satyr, swanmay, thri\_
kreen, and wemic. The kit is open to all classes.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Soldiers can be proficient in weapons that fit their adventuring class.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  History (ancient or local, emphasis on military events), fire\_building, direction sense, animal handling, cooking, heraldry, riding (land\_
based), seamanship, swimming, disguise, armorer, blind\_fighting, bowyer/fletcher, charioteering, endurance, navigation, survival, weaponsmithing, reading/writing.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  Soldiers must spend more than half of their starting wealth on weapons and armor. They can wear any armor and use any weapons allowed their adventuring profession.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits:}{\fs24  Keen eyesight, keen hearing, light sleeper.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  At the time of character creation, the soldier gains one free nonweapon proficiency, provided it is selected from the recommended list above. In addition, a soldier acquires one weapon at no cost\emdash 
a gift of the military.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  A soldier can purchase only those weapons, armor, and other pieces of equipment that can be carried on his back or on his mount.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Standard for the character\rquote s class.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Spy}{\fs24   In any campaign there is bound to be intrigue, conspiracies, and insidious plots. Uncovering these secrets is the job of the spy. A
s the scout crosses enemy lines and infiltrates dangerous wilderness areas to learn vital information, so does the spy wend his way through all levels of society. He attends parties or sits in smoky taverns, drinking, dancing, or gambling\emdash 
all the while noting who is speaking to whom, what is being discussed, (and what isn\rquote t being discussed), who\rquote s present and who\rquote 
s absent, and what are the latest rumors and gossip. Spies often choose to become adventurers, as that profession is the perfect cover. Few think twice when a new group of heroes comes into town. In most campaigns, adventurers are just accepted\emdash 
if not granted a few favors in case their unique talents are ever needed. This allows spies to go almost anywhere without arising suspicion.
\par \tab If this kit is chosen, the player should discuss with the DM exactly who the spy is supposed to be spying on, and who the spy\rquote 
s employer is. It is possible that the spy is between missions, or is seeking additional income as an adventurer. Spies tend to be educated and versatile. Roll 2d6 to determine the social rank of a spy character.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 4\tab Lower Middle Class
\par \tab 5\endash 12\tab Upper Middle Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24  Spies must be able to think on their feet and smoothly talk their way out of situations in which their cove
r may be blown. Therefore, all spies must have minimum Intelligence/Reason and Charisma/Appearance scores of 13. The spy kit is barred to half\_
ogres, as well as any optional PC race that is size large (restriction z from the Other Races section of Chapter Three), and thri\_kreen. The kit is open to all classes.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Spies are only limited in weapon choice by their adventuring class. However, depending on their cover identity, spies may be limited in the weapons they carry. For example, a sp
y impersonating a wizard cannot bring his favorite bastard sword along as part of the disguise. Many spies prefer small, easily concealed, or easily disguised weapons (a walking cane can double as a club or hide a thin blade). This lets spies defend thems
elves if they are discovered. Such weapons include: club, dagger, knife, and darts. If the campaign involves much courtly intrigue, \ldblquote ceremonial\rdblquote  weapons such as various swords also qualify. 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Disguise, forgery, dancing, etiquette, heraldry, riding, local history, modern languages, herbalism, reading/writing, appraising, gaming, musical instrument, reading lips, spellcraft.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  The spy\rquote s cover identity may dictate what equipment he can carry without appearing incongruous.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits:}{\fs24  Alertness, empathy, glibness, impersonation, light sleeper, lucky, music (any), precise memory.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  Because of the spy\rquote s suave charm, he receives a +2 bonus for all NPCs\rquote  reaction rolls.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  The foremost problem with being a spy is that if his cover identity is blown and he is captured, the penalty is often death. It is also possible that old foes may later determine the spy\rquote 
s true identity and seek revenge against him.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Standard for the character\rquote s class.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Swashbuckler}{\fs24   A swashbuckler is the lightly armed and armored hero with a flashing blade and rapier wit. Most at home in a city\_based campaign (dungeons tend to ruin their expensive, foppish attire), the swashbuckler s
eems to have stepped right out of Dumas\rquote  }{\i\fs24 The Three Musketeers.}{\fs24  Swashbucklers may be dull bureaucrats, wheezing aristocrats, or meek scribes during the day\emdash 
but at night they don their fanciest clothes, gird on their blades, and venture out into the darkening city for an evening of raucous revelry, flippant conversation, and a helping of derring\_
do. Despite a generally flippant (some might say arrogantly sarcastic) attitude, most swashbucklers have a strong sense of honor. Anyone who dares insult the swashbuckler, his comrades, or a member of the opposite sex may well find a blade at his throat.

\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Swashbucklers are more affluent than common fighters. Roll 2d6 to determine a social rank.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx1440\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 7\tab Upper Middle Class
\par \tab 8\endash 12\tab Upper Class
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24 
 Swashbucklers should be witty, and they should be light on their feet for those occasions when their wit goes unappreciated. As such, all swashbucklers need minimum Dexterity/Balance and Intelligence/Reason scores of 12. This kit is closed to 
the optional player character races, and to rangers and druids.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Swords are the traditional weapons of most swashbucklers, especially rapiers and sabres. However, they can use any weapons that fit their adventuring professions.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended Nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Tumbling, etiquette, dancing, riding, blind\_fighting, musical instrument, appraising, gaming, tightrope walking, jumping.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  A swashbuckler can wear any armor and weapons allowed to his class, though they should be flashy and of good quality.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits:}{\fs24  Ambidexterity, artistic ability, glibness, music, lucky.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  When unarmored or wearing armor no heavier than studded leather, swashbucklers gain a +2 armor class bonus. This bonus is in ad
dition to any other AC modifiers such as a high Dexterity and magical defensive items. (This is equal to the optional defensive bonus class ability, and the two cannot be combined.) Also, due to their roguish charm, swashbucklers gain a +2 reaction roll b
onus from NPC members of the opposite sex.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  As the swashbuckler seeks out adventure, so too does adventure find him. Life, and the DM, should conspire to make reality interesting for the character. For example, if a swashbuckler is fleeing the o
utraged sibling of one of his paramours, his flight might carry him across the path of a defenseless peasant who is being harassed by a gang of ruffians. Or, if a swashbuckler leaps overboard to escape the buccaneers who shanghaied him, he discovers that 
the waters are infested with sahuagin riding sharks.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Standard for the character\rquote s class, plus 20% to reflect his affluent background.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Thug}{\fs24   This kit is recommended only for non good\_aligned characters. As the outlaw makes his home and living on th
e fringes of civilization, the thug makes his way in cities. A thug knows the streets and alleys, and he knows the shadowy places best. In the darkest parts of town he lies in wait for unsuspecting prey\emdash 
such as travelers new to the city, those who become l
ost in the maze of streets and alleys, and those on their way home after having a bit too much to drink. Thugs might be street ruffians, members of press gangs, gang enforcers, or barroom rowdies. In any case, they tend to be loud, obnoxious, and prone to
 violence.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Most thugs come from low\_income families, and it is this background that leads them to their questionable lifestyle. Roll 2d6 to determine a thug\rquote s social rank.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 8\tab Lower Class
\par \tab 9\endash 12\tab Lower Middle Class
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommendations:}{\fs24  Thugs must have minimum Strength/Muscle and Charisma/Appearance scores of 10. This is barred to aarakocra, kobolds, satyrs, and swanmays. Only fighters and thieves may select this kit.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Small or easily concealed weapons are the thug\rquote 
s favorites, as they can be carried most anywhere. These include: daggers, dirks, hand axes, knives, blowguns, and slings. However, thugs can carry any weapon that is allowed by their adventuring class.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Blind\_fighting, endurance, gaming, jumping, rope use, running.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  Most thugs like to wear as little armor and carry as few pieces of equipment as possible\emdash as they like to move about unencumbered. However, some thugs prefer wearing bulky armor and carr
ying big weapons to intimidate others.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits: }{\fs24 Fast healer, keen eyesight, keen hearing, keen sense of touch, light sleeper.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  Due to their rough\_and\_tumble lifestyle and penchant for street brawls, thugs gain a +1 bonus to all damage rolls.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  Thugs are often wanted by the local law enforcers. In a relatively confined area such as a city, a thug can never relax. Around the next corner might be a member of the constabulary\emdash or the thug\rquote 
s next victim.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Standard for the character\rquote s class.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Weapon Master}{\fs24   A weapon master is a character who, regardless of class, has chosen to devote most of his combat training to a single weapon. A weapon master\rquote s goal is to blend weapon and self into one whirling, deadly union
\emdash one lethal entity. Weapon masters might display their prowess as part of a circus or carnival show\emdash knocking lit cigars out of unsuspecting peoples\rquote  mouths\emdash or juggling three short swords while blind\_
folded. Or, weapon masters might have inherited the family heirloom weapon, and mastering it is a family tradition that the character feels he must live up to.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Social ranks:}{\fs24  Weapon masters come from families that can afford weapon instructors. Roll 2d6 to determine a weapon master\rquote s social rank.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 2d6 roll\tab Rank
\par }{\fs24 \tab 2\endash 4\tab Lower Middle Class
\par \tab 5\endash 9\tab Upper Middle Class
\par \tab 10\endash 12\tab Upper Class
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements: }{\fs24 Only fighters, priests, and thieves can become weapon masters. Further, they must have minimum Strength/Stamina and Dexterity/Aim scores of 13. The kit is open to all races.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Weapon masters always choose a melee weapon to study and excel in. The character must begin play with expertise or specialization in at least one melee weapon.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Blind\_fighting, juggling, weaponsmithing, bowyer/fletcher, endurance.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment: }{\fs24 A weapon master cannot wear any armor heavier than chain mail. The weapon master begins play with one weapon ot the type he has mastered at no cost.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits:}{\fs24  Ambidexterity, double\_jointed, keen eyesight, keen hearing, lucky.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  A weapon master is so skilled with one melee weapon that he can, as he enters combat, display his amazing level of skill. This causes all opponents who see the display to suffer a \endash 
2 initiative penalty for the first two rounds of combat.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances: }{\fs24 So total is the weapon master\rquote s devotion to his weapon that he cannot become proficient with weapons of another type. For example, if the character\rquote 
s choice weapon is a mace, he can only gain proficiencies in bludgeoning weapons. If the chosen weapon is a sword, he can only wield slashing weapons without suffering penalties to hit.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Standard for the character\rquote s class.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Creating New Kits}{\fs24   To design additional kits, DMs should answer these questions.
\par \tab What role does this kit to serve in the campaign?
\par \tab What makes this unique from all other kits?
\par \tab In what ways is it similar to any other kits?
\par \tab What levels of society would generate characters that would choose this kit?
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements:}{\fs24  What does a character need to take this kit? What minimum of ability does this kit require? 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  Does this kit give access to weapons not normally allowed certain classes? If so, why? Are certain weapons associated with this kit? 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended nonweapon proficiencies:}{\fs24  What skills would PCs with this kit likely need?
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Equipment:}{\fs24  Does this kit grant access to equipment not normally available to some classes. If so, why?
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Recommended traits:}{\fs24  What characteristics fit an individual with this kit?
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Benefits:}{\fs24  What extra ability or modifier does this kit grant characters? Is this benefit too powerful for a kit? Is it too weak? Compare this kit\rquote s benefits with the other kit benefits in this chapter.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hindrances:}{\fs24  What penalty or modifier compensates for the above benefit without weakening the character too much or leaving him too powerful?
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wealth:}{\fs24  Is the starting cash of the character affect by this kit? If so, how much?
\par 
\par \tab Once these questions have been answered, a DM should have a pretty good idea of the compatibility of a new kit. The most important decision to make is determining if this kit unbalances the game. Contrast and compare it with other kits. It\rquote 
s much easier to fix the kit now than try to repair a campaign after an unbalanced kit has damaged it.
\par \tab The next 
step is to experiment with the kits in play. Outside the normal campaign, put this kit through its paces. Run different character classes through an encounter or two to see if it works the way it was intended. If it does, introduce it to campaign and have
 fun. If it doesn\rquote t, head back to the drawing board.
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Chapter 6:
\par Nonweapon Proficiencies
\par }{\fs24 
\par Nonweapon proficiencies are an important part of role\_playing a character. The Skills and Powers book offers several new proficiencies and ways in which they can be employed during a game. The new rules are tailored to the character point system.
\par \tab The use of proficiencies has been modified slightly from straight ability checks, providing more flexibility and a little more advantage to characters with less than, say, a
 15 or 16 score in a certain ability. Now proficiencies can develop during the course of a game. Though characters will often start out with less likelihood of success when using proficiencies, they will have many opportunities to develop those skills bey
ond the limits inherent in the old ability score system.
\par \tab In addition, players can select or create traits for their characters, which are innate advantages such as talents and keen perceptions. A list of traits is presented in this chapter. Countering the 
strengths of the traits is a selection of disadvantages, which also provide opportunities for more elaborate role\_playing\emdash as well as creating countless situations for a little comic relief.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Proficiencies and the Point System}{\fs24   The point system lets players give their characters a wider variety of skills and abilities than are available with the standard rules. However, skills that are not related to a character\rquote 
s class are still more difficult and expensive to obtain than those tied directly to the adventurer\rquote s area of expertise.
\par \tab Character points help determine an adventurer\rquote s initial proficiencies. Players can also assign character points earned during play to improve their heroes\rquote  chances of success with proficiencies, as well as to add new proficiencies.

\par \tab Unlike the original proficiency rules, the }{\i\fs24 Skills and Powers}{\fs24  system gives each proficiency a rating\emdash a possibility for success. This is influenced by a character\rquote 
s ability score (see Table 44: Ability Modifiers to Proficienc
y Scores). It is a more realistic approach, since proficiencies can be gradually improved. In these rules there is a significant difference in performance between someone with a beginning level of proficiency and someone who is an expert.
\par 
\par 
\par \tab In this step of the character creation process, all player characters receive an additional allotment of character points, based on their classes, to purchase their initial non\_
weapon proficiencies. In addition, any leftover points from race or class ability selection i
n the previous chapters can be used here. Finally, characters may add their CP bonuses for their Intelligence scores to their total of character points at this time. The number of CPs awarded to each type of character in this step appears in the table bel
ow.
\par \tab Characters may also choose disadvantages at this point, which can give them bonus CPs to spend in acquiring extra skills or traits.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24 Warriors:\tab 6
\par Wizards:\tab 8
\par Priests:\tab 8
