ation. The user can change himself into anything with approximately the same mass as his body: a wolf, a chair, or even a tree. While in this form, the user retains his own hit points and THAC0, but he gains the AC of the new form. He also gains all physi
cal attacks the form allows, but no magical or special abilities. A new attack ability depends on the form chosen; a tree, for example, can\rquote 
t attack, so it has no THAC0. Nonmagical movement is also gained. If the user metamorphs into another character race, use the }{\i\fs24 Monstrous Manual}{\fs24  book\rquote s descriptions for that race.
\par \tab Some forms have intrinsic advantages. Changing into a fish or rock renders the user immune to drowning, though he doesn\rquote t retain any senses not normally associated with his new form. He may opt 
to keep some of his own senses when he transforms, but these are likely to give him away.
\par \tab Like any massive change of shape, metamorphosis causes great physical stress. The user must make a system shock roll. If the roll fails, he expends 6 PSPs, changes form only for 1 round, and immediately passes out for 2d6 turns.
